    ///      it is not an escape from the claim-fee rate. `withdraw` runs `_syncRange` like every
    ///      other door, and that sync takes the claim-fee cut at whatever rate the range's snapshot
    ///      stands at — with no `maxFeeBps` to refuse it. So a caller who refuses a rate here and
    ///      then withdraws pays exactly the rate they refused. That is not a hole to plug: v4 hands
    ///      back accrued fees on EVERY `modifyLiquidity`, so there is no withdrawal that does not
    ///      collect, and a bound on `withdraw` could therefore only ever block an exit — which
    ///      inverts the one priority this contract will not trade. What bounds the exposure instead
    ///      is that the rate can only ever FALL over a given backlog: `claimFeeFor` bills the minimum
    ///      advertised across the range's own window, so `effectiveClaimFee(id)` is a hard ceiling
    ///      on what THIS withdrawal will pay.
    ///
    ///      NOT MONOTONE ACROSS TIME, and that changed this round. `_effectiveClaimFee` now returns
    ///      0 for a FULLY STAKED range, because `_split` skips the skim entirely there — so any
    ///      other holder's `unstake` restores the taxable base and the next reading RISES. Every
    ///      guarantee the two consent guards rest on holds within a single call, which is all they
    ///      need; a keeper caching the reading across blocks on the old monotonicity would be wrong.
    /// @dev WHAT EACH KIND OF LP GETS, since the three differ and the difference is the point:
    ///      - An UNSTAKED Fables LP receives their principal plus their accrued fees, already net
    ///        of the claim fee. That cut was taken at accrual, at the rate in force when the range
    ///        was last synced, so this pays what is owed and never re-charges.
    ///      - A STAKED Fables LP gets exactly what a plain `withdraw` gives them. Their fees went
    ///        to the distributor when they accrued — that is the trade they took in exchange for
    ///        emissions — so `owed0`/`owed1` hold only the unstaked slice, and for a fully staked
    ///        position that is zero. This function does NOT revert in that case; it simply pays
    ///        nothing extra, which is why the payout below is guarded rather than asserted.
    ///      - A vanilla Uniswap LP never reaches this contract at all.
    /// @dev PAUSABLE, and that is the whole point of the modifier. This function pays fees, and a
    ///      fee payout is exactly what the freeze exists to stop — the pause's own NatSpec says it
    ///      "freezes deposits, claims, staking, transfers and sweeps". Composing an unpausable
    ///      principal path with a pausable fee path inherits NEITHER guard unless one is written
    ///      here, and without it any holder drained their whole `owed` mid-incident for the price
    ///      of burning ONE liquidity unit. The asymmetry that made it worse than a nicety: the two
    ///      sweeps' PERMISSIONLESS doors are pause-gated, so during a freeze nobody outside could
    ///      move protocol revenue while every LP could still pull theirs. (`_settleSinks` is the
    ///      deliberate exception: it calls the INTERNAL sweeps, which carry no pause check, so an
    ///      admin rotating a sink still settles the outgoing one during a freeze — rotation has to
    ///      stay deterministic, which is the whole reason `_settleSinks` exists. It is not a door
    ///      anyone else can open.) And `_payout` burns the hook's ERC-6909
    ///      claims, which are keyed by CURRENCY and shared across every pool on the singleton, so
    ///      a drain in a contained pool drew down the claims backing the others. Eleven independent
    ///      audit lenses reported this; do not remove the modifier on the grounds that `withdraw`
    ///      does not carry one. `withdraw` moves no fees.
    /// @param maxFeeBps The highest claim-fee rate the caller will accept, mirroring `claimFees`.
    ///        Pass 10_000 to opt out of the bound entirely.
    function withdrawAndClaim(
        PoolKey calldata key,
        int24 tickLower,
        int24 tickUpper,
        uint128 liquidity,
        address to,
        uint128 amount0Min,
        uint128 amount1Min,
        uint256 deadline,
        uint16 maxFeeBps
    ) external nonReentrant whenPoolNotPaused(key.toId()) {
        // Checked BEFORE the withdrawal's own `_syncRange`, exactly as `claimFees` checks it before
        // its sync: the rate that will be charged is the range's snapshot, and consenting to it
        // after the fact is not consent. This is the one place this function is stricter than
        // `withdraw`, and the NatSpec above says why that is acceptable here and would not be there.
        //
        // ONE CONSEQUENCE OF THE PAUSE MODIFIER, stated so nobody later "fixes" it the wrong way.
        // The modifier fires before this check, so during a freeze the bound is unreachable and the
        // only exit is `withdraw` — which has no bound and still runs the claim-fee skim, because
        // `LedgerLib._onModify` credits fees unconditionally: the collection IS the
        // `modifyLiquidity`, not the `_syncRange` before it. So a freeze can cost an LP the option
        // of waiting for a rate cut. That is not a regression and it is not a reason to bound
        // `withdraw`: the skim happens on collection whichever door is used, `effectiveClaimFee` is
        // a min-over-window that can only fall and is capped by `MAX_CLAIM_FEE_BPS`, a pause is
        // bounded by `MAX_PAUSE`, and the LP can simply wait it out and use the bounded door.
        // Bounding `withdraw` would instead make principal exit refusable, which is the founding
        // constraint this whole file is built around.
        //
        // Note the option IS exclusively the LP's during a freeze, unlike in normal operation
        // where any stranger's one-unit `deposit` forces the sync and closes the window: `deposit`
        // is itself `whenPoolNotPaused`, so while a pool is frozen only the holder's own
        // `withdraw`/`unstake` — or a voter-gated `syncRanges` on a staked range — can force it.
        // That makes the freeze the one moment the timing is theirs alone, which strengthens the
        // case for gating the bounded door rather than weakening it.
        uint256 id = rangeId(key.toId(), tickLower, tickUpper);
        if (_effectiveClaimFee(id) > maxFeeBps) revert ClaimFeeAboveMax();

        // Principal first, fees second, and the order is load-bearing. `_withdrawTo` runs
        // `_syncRange` + `_settleUser`, so by the time it returns, `owed0`/`owed1` already hold
        // everything this holder earned right up to this block — including the fees collected by
        // the withdrawal's own touch. Claiming first would leave that last slice unpaid.
        _withdrawTo(key, tickLower, tickUpper, liquidity, to, amount0Min, amount1Min, deadline);

        UserPosition storage position = _positions[id][msg.sender];
        (uint256 amount0, uint256 amount1) = (position.owed0, position.owed1);
        if (amount0 == 0 && amount1 == 0) return; // fully staked, or nothing accrued: not an error
        (position.owed0, position.owed1) = (0, 0);
        // No cut here: it was taken at accrual. Same as `claimFees`.
        _payout(key.currency0, key.currency1, amount0, amount1, to);
        emit FeesClaimed(msg.sender, id, amount0, amount1);
    }

    /// @dev The body of `withdraw`, extracted so `withdrawAndClaim` can compose it. Both callers
    ///      are `nonReentrant`, and OpenZeppelin's transient guard cannot be re-entered from
    ///      within itself, so one external entry point cannot call the other — hence the split.
    ///      Behaviour is unchanged from when this was `withdraw`'s body.
    function _withdrawTo(
        PoolKey calldata key,
        int24 tickLower,
        int24 tickUpper,
        uint128 liquidity,
        address to,
        uint128 amount0Min,
        uint128 amount1Min,
        uint256 deadline
    ) internal {
        if (liquidity == 0) revert ZeroLiquidity();
        if (to == address(0)) revert ZeroAddress();
        _checkPayoutTarget(to);
        // forge-lint: disable-next-line(block-timestamp)
        if (block.timestamp > deadline) revert DeadlineExpired();
        _checkKey(key);
        uint256 id = rangeId(key.toId(), tickLower, tickUpper);

        _syncRange(id);
        _settleUser(id, msg.sender);

        UserPosition storage position = _positions[id][msg.sender];
        // balances are sums of uint128 liquidity mints, bounded by pool max liquidity
        // forge-lint: disable-next-line(unsafe-typecast)
        uint128 unstaked = uint128(balanceOf[msg.sender][id]) - position.staked;
        if (unstaked < liquidity) {
            uint128 fromStaked = liquidity - unstaked;
            position.staked -= fromStaked; // reverts on underflow: not enough total shares
            _ranges[id].totalStaked -= fromStaked;
            emit Unstaked(msg.sender, id, fromStaked);
        }
        _burn(msg.sender, id, liquidity);
        _ranges[id].totalShares -= liquidity;
        // Crossing DOWN to zero empties the position, clearing its ticks and re-basing
        // `feeGrowthInside`. Bumped BEFORE the pool touch so the collection it may trigger carries
        // the new value (H2), pairing with the up-crossing bump in `deposit`. No baseline emission
        // here, deliberately: the ticks are about to be cleared, so a reading taken now describes a
        // base no future collection will ever be in — the anchor worth having is the refill's, and
        // `deposit` emits it. Nothing accrues while the position is empty, so the seam carries no value.
        if (_ranges[id].totalShares == 0) _ranges[id].resetSeq += 1;
        _modify(key, tickLower, tickUpper, -int256(uint256(liquidity)), to, amount0Min, amount1Min);
        emit Withdrawn(msg.sender, id, liquidity);
    }

    /// @notice Syncs the range's fees from the pool and pays the caller's accrued share, minus
    ///         the claim fee (fees only — principal is never touched).
    /// @param to Recipient of the fees; lets a holder route around a token that blocks their
    ///        own address without stranding the other currency.
    // State is written after `_syncRange` -> poolManager.unlock, which Slither reads as
    // reentrancy. Every one of these paths carries OZ's ReentrancyGuardTransient, whose
    // tstore-based guard Slither does not model. Proven rather than asserted by
    // test_reentrancyGuardStopsAReEntryMidCallback, which re-enters from a hostile token's
    // transferFrom mid-callback and asserts the revert is ReentrancyGuardReentrantCall.
    // slither-disable-next-line reentrancy-no-eth
    function claimFees(PoolKey calldata key, int24 tickLower, int24 tickUpper, address to, uint16 maxFeeBps)
        external
        nonReentrant
        whenPoolNotPaused(key.toId())
    {
        // Mirrors `withdraw`. On an ERC20 pool a zero recipient reverts inside the token; on a
        // NATIVE pool v4's transfer is a bare `call`, which SUCCEEDS against address(0) and destroys
        // the fees after this function has already zeroed what was owed. Same input, opposite
        // outcome by currency type, and the silent one is the unrecoverable one.
        if (to == address(0)) revert ZeroAddress();
        _checkPayoutTarget(to);
        // The rate the caller is willing to pay. Uniswap v4 accrues fees lazily, so the hook
        // only learns of them when a ledger operation syncs the range — which means the rate
        // applied is whatever stands at sync time, and an LP whose range has been quiet since
        // before a `setClaimFee` would otherwise pay the new rate on the whole backlog. Taking
        // the cut at accrual bounds that window to the last sync; this bounds it to consent.
        // Checks the rate this call will actually charge — the range's snapshot — not the live
        // rate. Comparing against `claimFeeBps` once the charge moved to the snapshot made the
        // guard read one variable and the skim another, so it permitted exactly the skims it was
        // meant to block (snapshot high, live zero) and reverted claims that would have cost
        // nothing (snapshot zero, live high).
        _checkKey(key);
        uint256 id = rangeId(key.toId(), tickLower, tickUpper);
        if (_effectiveClaimFee(id) > maxFeeBps) revert ClaimFeeAboveMax();
        // Third free-sync door, same shape as the zero-amount ones closed on stake/unstake and
        // transfer: an address with no position here still reached `_syncRange` before hitting
        // the `owed == 0` exit, letting a stranger force a range's rate snapshot forward. A
        // holder who has withdrawn but still has unclaimed fees must remain able to claim, so
        // the test is "has shares OR is owed something", not "has shares".
        UserPosition storage claimant = _positions[id][msg.sender];
        if (balanceOf[msg.sender][id] == 0 && claimant.owed0 == 0 && claimant.owed1 == 0) {
            revert NothingToClaim();
        }
        _syncRange(id);
        _settleUser(id, msg.sender);

        UserPosition storage position = _positions[id][msg.sender];
        (uint256 amount0, uint256 amount1) = (position.owed0, position.owed1);
        if (amount0 == 0 && amount1 == 0) return;
        (position.owed0, position.owed1) = (0, 0);

        // No cut here: it was already taken at accrual, at the rate in force when the range was
        // last synced, so a later change cannot reach back into what is already owed.
        _payout(key.currency0, key.currency1, amount0, amount1, to);
        emit FeesClaimed(msg.sender, id, amount0, amount1);
    }

    /// @notice Stakes receipts: their future fee accrual routes to the distributor.
    // State is written after `_syncRange` -> poolManager.unlock, which Slither reads as
    // reentrancy. Every one of these paths carries OZ's ReentrancyGuardTransient, whose
    // tstore-based guard Slither does not model. Proven rather than asserted by
    // test_reentrancyGuardStopsAReEntryMidCallback, which re-enters from a hostile token's
    // transferFrom mid-callback and asserts the revert is ReentrancyGuardReentrantCall.
    // slither-disable-next-line reentrancy-no-eth
    function stake(PoolKey calldata key, int24 tickLower, int24 tickUpper, uint128 shares, uint256 minGaugeWeight)
        external
        nonReentrant
        whenPoolNotPaused(key.toId())
    {
        if (shares == 0) revert ZeroShares();
        _checkKey(key);
        // A desk that is not a LIVE gauge — killed, retired, or never added at all — has no voters
        // and earns no emissions, so forgoing fees to it buys nothing and strands the money: it
        // reaches the distributor with nobody entitled to claim it and sits there until the release
        // window 26 weeks later. `isGaugeLive` folds the class check together with the kill/retire
        // state: a never-gauged pool reads class NONE while `isGaugeKilled` alone returns false (the
        // LIVE default of an empty history), so the kill check by itself lets a stake strand into a
        // pool that was configured on the hook but never gauged — the exact gap FablesBribes closes
        // with `gaugeClass != NONE`. Staking is the only side that closes — unstaking, withdrawing,
        // claiming and ordinary LPing all stay open, because a wind-down must never trap anyone.
        // `isGaugeEarning`, not `isGaugeLive`. The comment above states the condition this guard is
        // for — "has no voters and earns no emissions, so forgoing fees to it buys nothing and
        // strands the money" — and `isGaugeLive` never tested the voters half of it: it is class +
        // kill state only. A LIVE, never-killed desk with no ballot pointed at it earns `_shareOf`
        // exactly zero while its stakers' fees are still swept to the distributor and booked to the
        // treasury, silently and indefinitely. `isGaugeEarning` adds the missing term.
        // FAILS CLOSED. An unset `ballotGate` used to make this guard a no-op, which is not only a
        // genesis condition: `ballotGate` is per-LEDGER, so every hook added to a running protocol
        // has its own, and between the new pool becoming depositable (`initialize` is permissionless)
        // and `setBallotGate` landing behind its config delay, staking was accepted on a desk with no
        // gauge at all. Fees swept, nothing earned, nobody told. Staking before the gate is wired
        // could never earn anyway — `_shareOf` needs a gauge — so refusing costs nothing real and
        // removes a window whose length is set by a multisig delay.
        address gate = ballotGate;
        if (gate == address(0) || !IBallotGate(gate).isGaugeEarning(key.toId())) revert DeskRetired();
        // THE CONSENT BOUND, and the reason the boolean above is not enough on its own.
        //
        // `isGaugeEarning` asks "is a ballot pointed here", and any address can make that true for
        // ONE WEI: `createPermanentLock(1)` books `bias = 1 * MAX_LOCK` with `slope == 0`, which
        // satisfies `bias > t * slope` forever. The two halves of the staker's trade then use
        // different denominators — the fees they give up are split among THIS desk's voters, so
        // the lone dust voter takes ~100% of them, while the emissions they receive are sized by
        // the desk's share of the GLOBAL vote, which rounds to zero. The gate's own NatSpec says it
        // exists to stop a staker "forgoing fees to it buys nothing"; a dust ballot satisfies the
        // gate and produces exactly that outcome, with the nominal voter collecting the proceeds.
        //
        // Fixed with a caller-supplied bound rather than a protocol-wide floor, deliberately. A
        // protocol floor needs either a magic constant — arbitrary, and wrong for some desk — or a
        // cross-gauge sum, which is gas every staker pays forever to protect a party who can state
        // their own terms in one argument. `claimFees` already establishes exactly this pattern
        // with `maxFeeBps`: the caller names the worst price they accept and the contract enforces
        // it. `0` means "no opinion" and preserves the old behaviour, the same way
        // `maxFeeBps = 10_000` opts out of the claim-fee bound today.
        //
        // Bounded on WEIGHT, not on emission share: share needs the epoch's global total, which is
        // only meaningful against a fully checkpointed frontier and would make this revert for
        // every desk at the start of every epoch. Weight is the same quantity `_shareOf` divides,
        // it is O(1) from two slots the gate already touches, and the caller compares it against a
        // total they can read off-chain.
        //
        // THAT CLAIM WAS ONCE FALSE OF THIS VERY LINE, which is worth recording because the fix
        // lives in another contract. `gaugeWeightNow` used to refuse to answer past its own
        // frontier's week — returning 0 rather than a stale number — so a bounded stake reverted
        // `DeskBelowMinWeight` on a FULLY BACKED desk for the whole pre-checkpoint part of every
        // epoch: exactly the fail-shut this paragraph says weight was chosen to avoid. The gate
        // now returns a CONSERVATIVE LOWER BOUND instead, and that is what makes this bound sound
        // in both directions: an un-applied expiry can only make the reading too LOW, so a stale
        // gauge can refuse a stake the caller would have accepted, never admit one into a desk
        // weaker than they demanded. See `FablesVoter.gaugeWeightNow`.
        //
        // The reading is therefore a floor, not a quote: `minGaugeWeight` says "refuse unless the
        // desk is at least this strong", and a desk that is stronger than it looks is still
        // refused. That direction is the caller's protection, so it is the right one to keep.
        //
        // ENTRY ONLY, and that is a real limit rather than an oversight: nothing re-checks it, so
        // an LP whose desk loses its backers later keeps forfeiting fees until they `unstake` —
        // which is always available and never pausable. Re-validating on every collection would put
        // a cross-contract call on the hot path of every swap-adjacent sync. The signal belongs in
        // the app. `Staked` does NOT carry the weight — it is `(owner, rangeId, shares)` — so an
        // indexer raising that signal must read `IBallotGate.gaugeWeightNow(poolId)` itself — and
        // `Staked` carries `rangeId`, not `poolId`, so it must go through `rangeKey(id)` first. This
        // line claimed the event carried it, which made the only stated mitigation for a deliberate
        // design gap point at a field that does not exist.
        // Called ONLY when a bound is set. `minGaugeWeight` is a parameter, not storage, so an
        // unconditional call would make every staker on every pool depend on a gate member that
        // nothing had checked exists — which is exactly the silent-brick class `setBallotGate`'s
        // probe was built for, and which an unconditional first cut of this line re-opened.
        // Skipping the call at zero also keeps the no-opinion path at its old gas.
        if (minGaugeWeight != 0 && IBallotGate(gate).gaugeWeightNow(key.toId()) < minGaugeWeight) {
            revert DeskBelowMinWeight();
        }
        uint256 id = rangeId(key.toId(), tickLower, tickUpper);
        _syncRange(id); // fees earned before this stake stay with their earner
        _settleUser(id, msg.sender);
        UserPosition storage position = _positions[id][msg.sender];
        if (uint128(balanceOf[msg.sender][id]) - position.staked < shares) revert InsufficientUnstakedShares();
        position.staked += shares;
        _ranges[id].totalStaked += shares;
        emit Staked(msg.sender, id, shares);
    }

    /// @notice Unstakes receipts; permissionless, instant, dependency-free.
    /// @dev Deliberately NOT pausable, unlike `stake`. Unstaking only ever SHRINKS what the
    ///      distributor is owed and makes no external call, so it cannot be an exploit vector —
    ///      while pausing it would mean an LP cannot stop routing their fees to a distributor
    ///      the admin also chooses. Freezing the exit from a revenue assignment is not an
    ///      exploit response, it is the exploit.
    // State is written after `_syncRange` -> poolManager.unlock, which Slither reads as
    // reentrancy. Every one of these paths carries OZ's ReentrancyGuardTransient, whose
    // tstore-based guard Slither does not model. Proven rather than asserted by
    // test_reentrancyGuardStopsAReEntryMidCallback, which re-enters from a hostile token's
    // transferFrom mid-callback and asserts the revert is ReentrancyGuardReentrantCall.
    // slither-disable-next-line reentrancy-no-eth
    function unstake(PoolKey calldata key, int24 tickLower, int24 tickUpper, uint128 shares) external nonReentrant {
        // A zero-share call did real work: it forced a full fee sync for free, from any address,
        // with no balance and no approval, and is not pausable. That was the cheapest trigger for
        // the claim-fee raid, and it also let anyone spam the Staked/Unstaked stream the
        // settlement indexer reads.
        if (shares == 0) revert ZeroShares();
        _checkKey(key);
        uint256 id = rangeId(key.toId(), tickLower, tickUpper);
        _syncRange(id); // fees owed to the distributor are banked before the stake shrinks
        _settleUser(id, msg.sender);
        UserPosition storage position = _positions[id][msg.sender];
        position.staked -= shares; // reverts on underflow
        _ranges[id].totalStaked -= shares;
        emit Unstaked(msg.sender, id, shares);
    }

    /// @notice Pushes accumulated staked-share fees to the distributor. Callable by anyone.
    function sweepDistributor(Currency currency) external nonReentrant whenNotPaused {
        _sweepDistributor(currency);
    }

    /// @dev A thin wrapper over `LedgerLib.sweepDistributor` (inlined).
    ///      FF-denominated staked-fee revenue does NOT go to the epoch distributor — which treats
    ///      every FF it holds as the emission tranche, where fee revenue would strand — but to the
    ///      dedicated `ffFeeSink`; both destinations are resolved inside the library, and an unset
    ///      one simply keeps the accrual waiting. Virtual because the distributor speaks only
    ///      ERC20: the native-capable hook overrides the NATIVE currency's sweep to wrap into WETH
    ///      at this boundary, so everything downstream stays ERC20-pure.
    function _sweepDistributor(Currency currency) internal virtual {
        LedgerLib.sweepDistributor(distributorOwed, poolManager, currency, distributor, distributorFF, ffFeeSink);
    }

    /* ---------------------------------- transfers ---------------------------------- */

    /// @dev Staked shares are locked: only the unstaked balance is transferable.
    function transfer(address receiver, uint256 id, uint256 amount)
        public
        override
        nonReentrant
        whenRangeNotPaused(id)
        returns (bool)
    {
        // Same free, permissionless fee-sync primitive that was closed on stake/unstake: a
        // zero-amount transfer needs no balance and no approval, moves nothing, and still forces
        // a full unlock + modifyLiquidity round trip against any range.
        if (amount == 0) revert ZeroShares();
        _checkTransferable(msg.sender, id, amount);
        _syncRange(id); // the sender keeps the fees their shares already earned
        _settleUser(id, msg.sender);
        _settleUser(id, receiver);
        return super.transfer(receiver, id, amount);
    }

    function transferFrom(address sender, address receiver, uint256 id, uint256 amount)
        public
        override
        nonReentrant
        whenRangeNotPaused(id)
        returns (bool)
    {
        // Same free, permissionless fee-sync primitive that was closed on stake/unstake: a
        // zero-amount transfer needs no balance and no approval, moves nothing, and still forces
        // a full unlock + modifyLiquidity round trip against any range.
        if (amount == 0) revert ZeroShares();
        _checkTransferable(sender, id, amount);
        _syncRange(id); // the sender keeps the fees their shares already earned
        _settleUser(id, sender);
        _settleUser(id, receiver);
        // `sender` is not `msg.sender` BY DESIGN: this IS ERC-6909's delegated transfer, and the
        // authorization lives one frame down. v4-core's `ERC6909.transferFrom` accepts only
        // `msg.sender == sender`, `isOperator[sender][msg.sender]`, or a checked
        // `allowance[sender][msg.sender][id] -= amount` that reverts on underflow — and this
        // contract overrides none of `approve`, `setOperator`, `isOperator`, `allowance`. The
        // rule's premise (an arbitrary `from` with no allowance check) is therefore absent, and
        // these are the hook's OWN receipt shares, not an external ERC-20 balance. Verified by
        // attacking it three ways — no approval, one wei over the allowance, and a self-appointed
        // operator — each refused, with positive controls proving the fixture could have succeeded.
        // nosemgrep: solidity.security.arbitrary-send-erc20.arbitrary-send-erc20
        return super.transferFrom(sender, receiver, id, amount);
    }

    function _checkTransferable(address owner, uint256 id, uint256 amount) internal view {
        if (balanceOf[owner][id] - _positions[id][owner].staked < amount) revert InsufficientUnstakedShares();
    }

    /* ---------------------------------- internals ---------------------------------- */

    // `Action`, `CallbackData` and `PayoutData` now live in `LedgerTypes.sol` (shared with
    // `LedgerLib.onUnlock`, which holds the unlock-callback body).

    function _modify(
        PoolKey calldata key,
        int24 tickLower,
        int24 tickUpper,
        int256 liquidityDelta,
        address user,
        uint128 amount0Limit,
        uint128 amount1Limit
    ) internal {
        _modifyMem(key, tickLower, tickUpper, liquidityDelta, user, amount0Limit, amount1Limit);
    }

    function _modifyMem(
        PoolKey memory key,
        int24 tickLower,
        int24 tickUpper,
        int256 liquidityDelta,
        address user,
        uint128 amount0Limit,
        uint128 amount1Limit
    ) internal {
        poolManager.unlock(
            abi.encode(
                Action.MODIFY,
                abi.encode(CallbackData(key, tickLower, tickUpper, liquidityDelta, user, amount0Limit, amount1Limit))
            )
        );
    }

    /// @dev Burns the ledger's claims and pays `to` in real tokens, via `LedgerLib.payout`
    ///      (inlined). A failure here (paused or blocklisted token, blocked recipient) affects
    ///      only this claim, never principal.
    function _payout(Currency currency0, Currency currency1, uint256 amount0, uint256 amount1, address to) internal {
        LedgerLib.payout(poolManager, currency0, currency1, amount0, amount1, to);
    }

    /// @inheritdoc IUnlockCallback
    /// @dev A thin shell: it checks the caller and hands the whole callback body to
    ///      `LedgerLib.onUnlock` (inlined), which decodes the action, applies a liquidity
    ///      modify or a payout, and settles fees and principal.
    function unlockCallback(bytes calldata rawData) external returns (bytes memory) {
        if (msg.sender != address(poolManager)) revert NotPoolManager();
        return LedgerLib.onUnlock(
            _ranges,
            _rangeKeys,
            distributorOwed,
            claimFeeOwed,
            claimFeeSyncIndex,
            _poolClaimFee,
            poolManager,
            LedgerLib.ClaimFeeState({recipient: claimFeeRecipient}),
            rawData
        );
    }

    /// @dev The whole fee collection — claim-fee rate roll-forward, the claim mints, the staked /
    ///      unstaked split, the claim-fee skim and the `FeesCollected` emit — lives in
    ///      `LedgerLib.creditFees` (inlined), which mutates this contract's own storage
    ///      (mappings passed by reference). Behaviour is identical.
    function _creditFees(uint256 id, PoolKey memory key, uint128 fee0, uint128 fee1) internal {
        LedgerLib.creditFees(
            _ranges,
            _rangeKeys,
            distributorOwed,
            claimFeeOwed,
            claimFeeSyncIndex,
            _poolClaimFee,
            poolManager,
            LedgerLib.CreditParams({id: id, key: key, fee0: fee0, fee1: fee1, claimFeeRecipient: claimFeeRecipient})
        );
    }

    /// @notice Realizes v4's lazily-accrued fees for a list of ranges, splitting each between its
    ///         unstaked holders and the voters' pot. Callable only by a VOTER of the pool, on the
    ///         strength of a lock they own; unpausable, and it
    ///         moves no tokens out of this contract — it only turns fees the pool already holds
    ///         into balances this ledger has attributed.
    /// @dev Settlement needs this. v4 pays nothing until a position is touched, and a `rangeId`
    ///      is `(pool, tickLower, tickUpper)`, so an LP on bespoke bounds is the ONLY party who
    ///      can touch their range. Staked shares give their fees up to voters, which left
    ///      voters' revenue hostage to whether the LP who forgoes it ever felt like syncing —
    ///      and left each epoch's emission split guessing at fees that had not been booked yet.
    ///      Ops calls this over the staked ranges at each epoch boundary, which is also what
    ///      makes the `FeesCollected` accumulator levels line up with epoch boundaries so the
    ///      week's forgone fees are attributable exactly.
    /// @dev It takes the claim fee like every other door, and it must. An earlier version skipped
    ///      the skim here on the grounds that "the protocol forgoes its claim fee on syncs it did
    ///      not initiate — it still collects on every owner-driven path". The second clause was
    ///      false. v4 pays nothing until a position is touched, so an owner-driven sync can only
    ///      ever skim fees accrued SINCE THE LAST TOUCH — and a free door that collects the whole
    ///      backlog sets that to nothing. Whoever touches a range last wins, so any voter of the
    ///      pool could hold the protocol's revenue on every staked range in it at zero forever,
    ///      for gas, by sweeping just before each owner claim. Charging uniformly is the only
    ///      shape with no door to arbitrage between.
    ///
    ///      It costs the caller nothing they are owed: the skim is taken from the UNSTAKED
    ///      residue, after `stakedCut` has already been set aside for the voters' pot. A voter
    ///      syncing to fund their own claim is not paying for the privilege.
    ///
    ///      The rate is still `min(live, snapshot)`, so a raise can never reach back over a
    ///      backlog earned under the old rate — which was the other thing the exemption was
    ///      protecting, and is protected here by `_effectiveClaimFee` instead of by a hole.
    /// @dev SCOPE, stated because four of the twelve round-2 lenses stopped here and one wrote a
    ///      passing PoC. This gate does NOT make the epoch-attribution choice exclusive to voters,
    ///      and nothing in this contract could: `deposit(key, tl, tu, 1, max, max, deadline)`
    ///      reaches the identical `_syncRange(id)` on the identical range for one liquidity unit,
    ///      with no gauge, ledger or ballot requirement — as does the range holder's own
    ///      `withdraw`/`claimFees`. So the timing power is public, and this gate restricts only
    ///      third-party pokes of ranges the caller has no position in.
    ///
    ///      Left as-is on purpose rather than tightened or dropped. Tightening (gating
    ///      `_syncRange` itself when a range has staked shares) would refuse a first-time
    ///      depositor into that range, which is a real user and not the threat. Dropping the gate
    ///      is a LOOSENING of an audited contract to remove an inconsistency that costs nothing,
    ///      and it would also strand `setBallotGate`'s `ballotBacks` probe, which exists because
    ///      this is one of the ledger's only two runtime calls into the gate.
    ///
    ///      What makes the residual acceptable is that the power itself is nearly inert. The
    ///      settlement indexer attributes fees from the `feeGrowthInside`/`resetSeq` levels carried
    ///      on `FeesCollected`, not from the block a collection happened in, so moving a
    ///      collection between weeks does not move the money. The one live edge is the claim-fee
    ///      window: a stranger syncing just before a scheduled rate CUT pins that range's backlog
    ///      at the pre-cut rate. That is bounded by `MAX_CLAIM_FEE_BPS`, can never charge more
    ///      than the rate the protocol was publicly advertising at the time (`claimFeeFor` takes
    ///      the minimum over the window), and is already characterized in `claimFeeFor`'s NatSpec.
    function syncRanges(uint256 tokenId, uint256[] calldata ids) external nonReentrant {
        address gate = ballotGate;
        if (gate == address(0)) revert BallotGateUnset();
        for (uint256 i = 0; i < ids.length; i++) {
            uint256 id = ids[i];
            // Staked ranges only. A range with nothing staked owes the voters nothing.
            if (_ranges[id].totalStaked == 0) continue;
            // And only a voter of THIS pool, on the strength of a lock they own. Collecting is
            // already harmless to principal — `_syncRange` passes `liquidityDelta == 0`, so it
            // can never move a position, and it merely books an unstaked holder's share into an
            // accumulator only they can draw down. The gate is not there to stop theft; it is
            // there because fees are attributed to the week they are COLLECTED in, so whoever
            // chooses the moment chooses the epoch. That choice belongs to the people the money
            // is owed to, not to whoever is watching.
            if (!IBallotGate(gate).ballotBacks(msg.sender, tokenId, _rangeKeys[id].key.toId())) {
                revert NotAVoterOfThisPool();
            }
            _syncRange(id);
        }
    }

    /// @notice Points the sync gate at the Voter. Until it is set, nothing can be synced through
    ///         this door at all — a closed door being the safe default for one that decides who
    ///         may reach into someone else's position.
    /// @dev Probed, not merely stored. `setDistributor` spends a `try/catch` on its counterpart
    ///      for exactly this reason and this setter did not, so an EOA or a wrong-ABI contract
    ///      made `syncRanges` revert on `abi.decode` for every staked range — permanently
    ///      unusable, with no on-chain signal. `address(0)` stays explicitly allowed: a closed
    ///      door is the safe default for one that decides who may reach into someone else's
    ///      position, and `syncRanges` refuses outright while the gate is unset.
    function setBallotGate(address gate) external restricted {
        if (gate != address(0)) {
            // Explicit, because `try/catch` alone does NOT cover this. Since solc 0.8.10 a
            // high-level call expecting return data skips the extcodesize check and lets the ABI
            // decoder revert instead — and that revert happens in the SUCCESS branch, so `catch`
            // never sees it. An EOA here would have produced a bare revert indistinguishable
            // from out-of-gas: the very "no on-chain signal" this setter is guarding against.
            if (gate.code.length == 0) revert InvalidBallotGate();
            try IBallotGate(gate).ballotBacks(address(this), 0, PoolId.wrap(bytes32(0))) returns (bool) {}
            catch {
                revert InvalidBallotGate();
            }
            // BOTH members, not just the one this contract happens to call first. `stake` calls
            // `isGaugeEarning`, so a gate implementing only `ballotBacks` passed this setter and
            // then made `stake` revert on the ABI decode for every pool at once — with no
            // on-chain signal, which is the exact failure the probe above exists to prevent. The
            // probe must track the member `stake` actually calls: it was `isGaugeLive` before the
            // guard moved to `isGaugeEarning`, and staying on the old member reopened the gap.
            try IBallotGate(gate).isGaugeEarning(PoolId.wrap(bytes32(0))) returns (bool) {}
            catch {
                revert InvalidBallotGate();
            }
            // The THIRD member `stake` reaches, probed for the same reason as the two above. It is
            // called only when a caller sets `minGaugeWeight`, so a gate missing it would brick not
            // every stake but every BOUNDED one — a rarer failure and therefore a quieter one, with
            // the same shape: passes this setter, reverts on the ABI decode later, no signal.
            try IBallotGate(gate).gaugeWeightNow(PoolId.wrap(bytes32(0))) returns (uint256) {}
            catch {
                revert InvalidBallotGate();
            }
            // AND THE GATE MUST BE THE VOTER THAT ESCROW ACTUALLY CALLS BACK. Every probe above
            // asks "can this address answer?"; none asks "is this the SAME Voter the escrow is
            // wired to?" — and nothing anywhere did. The Voter's anti-rental guarantee rests
            // entirely on `ve.voter() == address(voter)`, which `FablesVoter` never asserts: it
            // only checks `msg.sender == address(ve)` inside its hooks, which is a one-way test.
            //
            // The two can genuinely come apart. `VotingEscrowFF.setVoter` is WRITE-ONCE, while this
            // setter and a `FablesBribes` redeploy can both follow a Voter migration — and the
            // Phase-2 backlog already contains a `_shareOf` change that requires exactly such a
            // migration. Split them and borrow-vote-return works permanently and SILENTLY: the
            // escrow calls the old Voter, which holds no ballot for that token, so nothing reverts
            // and no event fires, while the new Voter keeps crediting the borrowed vote.
            //
            // Two lines now; impossible after `setVoter` is burned.
            try IBallotGate(gate).ve() returns (address escrow) {
                // `escrow.code.length` FIRST, and for the reason this function already states
                // twenty lines up: since solc 0.8.10 a high-level call expecting return data skips
                // the extcodesize check and lets the ABI decoder revert instead — and that revert
                // lands in the SUCCESS branch, where `catch` does not reach it. `escrow` is
                // attacker-influenced (it is whatever the gate chose to name), so without this an
                // EOA there propagates a bare decoder revert instead of `InvalidBallotGate`.
                //
                // Every branch still fails closed and `setBallotGate` is `restricted` and
                // re-callable, so this is diagnostics rather than a hole — but the rule is this
                // file's own, and the newest patch to it broke it.
                if (escrow == address(0) || escrow.code.length == 0) revert InvalidBallotGate();
                // RAW STATICCALL WITH A LENGTH CHECK, not `try/catch`, and the difference was
                // measured rather than assumed. `catch` covers a revert FROM the call; it does not
                // cover a failure to decode the call's RETURN DATA. So a contract with a payable
                // catch-all succeeds with empty returndata, the decoder reverts in the success
                // branch, and that propagates past both catches as a bare `revert(0,0)` — the very
                // "no on-chain signal" this setter exists to prevent, one shape further out than the
                // `code.length` check above.
                //
                // This is the pattern `HookFamily._answers` already states the rule for: check
                // `ret.length >= 32` rather than bare success, because a call into an address with
                // no matching function can succeed with empty returndata. One error covers all three
                // shapes — revert, empty/short returndata, wrong value.
                (bool ok, bytes memory ret) = escrow.staticcall(abi.encodeWithSignature("voter()"));
                if (!ok || ret.length < 32 || abi.decode(ret, (address)) != gate) revert InvalidBallotGate();
            } catch {
                revert InvalidBallotGate();
            }
        }
        ballotGate = gate;
        emit BallotGateSet(gate);
    }

    /// @dev The rate a sync of `id` will ACTUALLY charge: the range's snapshot, capped by the
    ///      policy standing now. A reduction binds immediately, so the snapshot is an upper
    ///      bound rather than a figure that can outlive the policy it came from; only an
    ///      increase waits for the range's next sync, which is what stops a raise reaching back
    ///      over a backlog earned entirely under the old rate.
    /// @dev Both the skim and `claimFees`'s consent guard read this one function. They used to
    ///      compute it separately, and drifted apart twice: the guard read the live rate while
    ///      the skim charged the snapshot, then the guard read the snapshot while the skim
    ///      charged the minimum. Each time the two disagreed the guard rejected claims that
    ///      would have cost nothing, or waved through skims it existed to block. One definition
    ///      is the only shape that cannot drift again.
    function _effectiveClaimFee(uint256 id) internal view returns (uint16) {
        // A FULLY STAKED RANGE IS CHARGED NOTHING, so the bound must say nothing too.
        //
        // The skim lives in `LedgerLib._split`'s `unstakedTotal != 0` path; the fully-staked branch
        // returns before it, routing the whole fee to the distributor. So at `totalStaked ==
        // totalShares` the claim fee actually charged is ZERO at any configured `bps` — while this
        // function still returned the pool's live rate, and the guards it feeds
        // (`claimFees`/`withdrawAndClaim` against `maxFeeBps`) then refused a claim that would have
        // cost the caller nothing. A caller passing `maxFeeBps = 0` — "charge me nothing" — was
        // turned away from exactly the case where nothing is charged.
        //
        // This function's own contract is that it equals what the next sync will charge; the rate
        // and the taxable base were separated when the skim moved onto the unstaked residue, and
        // only the rate was tracked. The direction was always safe (it over-stated, never under),
        // so this is correctness rather than solvency — but "safe" is not "right" on a guard whose
        // whole job is to be exact.
        // THE SKIM'S REAL PRECONDITION, which is `totalStaked == totalShares` — nothing more.
        // `_split` skips the claim-fee skim in TWO states: `unstakedTotal == 0` (fully staked) and
        // an EMPTY range, where `_syncRange` returns before `_split` is reached at all. The round-6
        // form carried a `totalShares != 0` conjunct that excluded the second, so an emptied range
        // with `owed` still booked reported the live rate and `claimFees(..., maxFeeBps = 0)` was
        // refused on exactly the case where nothing is charged. Three independent lenses PoC'd it.
        //
        // Dropping the conjunct only ever WIDENS acceptance in states where the skim is provably
        // zero (`0 == 0` for an empty range), so it cannot admit a skim that was previously refused.
        RangeState storage range = _ranges[id];
        if (range.totalStaked == range.totalShares) return 0;
        return claimFeeFor(_rangeKeys[id].key.toId(), claimFeeSyncIndex[id]);
    }

    /// @notice The rate a backlog that opened at history index `sinceIndex` will actually be
    ///         charged: the LOWEST rate that stood at any point from then until now.
    /// @dev The minimum, and not a time-weighted average, because a time-weighted average is not
    ///      computable here and pretending otherwise would be a guess dressed as arithmetic. v4
    ///      hands over one lump for the whole window and says nothing about WHEN inside it the
    ///      fees were earned — a week's backlog might all have accrued in one busy hour. Any
    ///      apportionment would be invented.
    ///
    ///      Given that, the minimum is the only defensible answer, for two reasons that matter
    ///      more than the revenue:
    ///
    ///        - It can never charge an LP a rate the protocol was not actually advertising while
    ///          their fees were accruing. Every other rule can.
    ///        - It bounds the timing game, though it does not remove it. Anyone can force a range
    ///          to realize its fees (`deposit` of one unit does it), so a third party picks the
    ///          moment a backlog is priced. Waiting can only ever LOWER the rate — so there is
    ///          nothing to gain by delaying. But a scheduled cut is public before it lands, and
    ///          collecting just BEFORE it closes the window at the old rate. An earlier version
    ///          of this comment claimed there was nothing to front-run; that is false, and the
    ///          exposure is bounded only by `MAX_CLAIM_FEE_BPS` and by the rate shipping at zero.
    ///
    ///      The cost is that ops must treat a dip as retroactive. Lowering the rate for an hour
    ///      applies that hour's rate to every backlog spanning it, however long. That is a real
    ///      constraint on the lever, and the right way round: it makes a cut generous rather than
    ///      a raise reaching backwards.
    function claimFeeFor(PoolId poolId, uint64 sinceIndex) public view returns (uint16) {
        PoolClaimFee storage pcf = _poolClaimFee[poolId];
        return LedgerLib.claimFeeFor(pcf.history, sinceIndex, claimFeeRecipient, pcf.bps, pcf.allTimeMin);
    }

    /// @notice This pool's current claim-fee rate.
    function claimFeeBps(PoolId poolId) external view returns (uint16) {
        return _poolClaimFee[poolId].bps;
    }

    /// @notice The lowest rate this POOL has ever stood at (the bounded walk's fallback).
    function claimFeeAllTimeMin(PoolId poolId) external view returns (uint16) {
        return _poolClaimFee[poolId].allTimeMin;
    }

    /// @notice Number of recorded claim-fee changes.
    function claimFeeHistoryLength(PoolId poolId) external view returns (uint256) {
        return _poolClaimFee[poolId].history.length;
    }

    /// @notice One recorded claim-fee change.
    function claimFeeChangeAt(PoolId poolId, uint256 index) external view returns (ClaimFeeChange memory) {
        return _poolClaimFee[poolId].history[index];
    }

    /// @notice The rate a range's current backlog will be charged if it is synced now.
    function effectiveClaimFee(uint256 id) external view returns (uint16) {
        return _effectiveClaimFee(id);
    }

    // `_creditFees` emits `FeesCollected` from inside `LedgerLib.creditFees` (inlined), which
    // also reads the range's live `feeGrowthInside` there; both used to be `_emitCollected` /
    // `_growthOf` here, factored into `LedgerLib` for organization.

    /// @dev A thin wrapper over `LedgerLib.settleUser` (inlined), so the per-holder fee and
    ///      emission-weight checkpoint math — this contract's other `FullMath.mulDiv` cluster —
    ///      lives in `LedgerLib`. Called from nine paths, all of which are cheap internal jumps.
    function _settleUser(uint256 id, address owner) internal {
        LedgerLib.settleUser(_ranges[id], _positions[id][owner], balanceOf[owner][id], owner, id);
    }

    /// @notice Pushes accrued claim-fee revenue to the recipient. Callable by anyone.
    function sweepClaimFee(Currency currency) external nonReentrant whenNotPaused {
        _sweepClaimFee(currency);
    }

    /// @dev A thin wrapper over `LedgerLib.sweepClaimFee` (inlined).
    /// @dev Virtual for the same reason `_sweepDistributor` is, and it was a defect that it was
    ///      not. The library's last hop is `poolManager.take(currency, claimFeeRecipient, amount)`,
    ///      which for NATIVE is a bare `call` in v4 — so on an ETH pool this pays raw ETH to an
    ///      address nothing checks is payable. `FablesTreasurySplitter`, the contract whose own
    ///      NatSpec names "ledger claim fees" as one of its three inputs, has no `receive`, no
    ///      `fallback` and no native accounting at all, so the sweep reverted `NativeTransferFailed`
    ///      permanently and the revenue sat as hook-held claims with no other exit.
    ///
    ///      Worse than the stranding: `_settleSinks` runs at the head of `setClaimFee`,
    ///      `setDistributor` and `setFFFeeSink`, so listing the native currency in `settleFirst` —
    ///      which is exactly what those setters tell operators to do — reverted the whole rotation,
    ///      taking the healthy distributor sweep down with it. Rotating away from the bricked sink
    ///      therefore required an EMPTY `settleFirst`, which silently re-points the already-accrued
    ///      native pot at the new recipient: the precise outcome `_settleSinks` exists to prevent.
    ///
    ///      The asymmetry was the tell. One sweep of a matched pair was given a native branch and
    ///      the other was not, on the unexamined belief that only the distributor speaks ERC20.
    function _sweepClaimFee(Currency currency) internal virtual {
        LedgerLib.sweepClaimFee(claimFeeOwed, poolManager, currency, claimFeeRecipient);
    }
}
"
    },
    "lib/v4-core/src/ERC6909.sol": {
      "content": "// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT
pragma solidity ^0.8.0;

import {IERC6909Claims} from "./interfaces/external/IERC6909Claims.sol";

/// @notice Minimalist and gas efficient standard ERC6909 implementation.
/// @author Solmate (https://github.com/transmissions11/solmate/blob/main/src/tokens/ERC6909.sol)
/// @dev Copied from the commit at 4b47a19038b798b4a33d9749d25e570443520647
/// @dev This contract has been modified from the implementation at the above link.
abstract contract ERC6909 is IERC6909Claims {
    /*//////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
                             ERC6909 STORAGE
    //////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////*/

    mapping(address owner => mapping(address operator => bool isOperator)) public isOperator;

    mapping(address owner => mapping(uint256 id => uint256 balance)) public balanceOf;

    mapping(address owner => mapping(address spender => mapping(uint256 id => uint256 amount))) public allowance;

    /*//////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
                              ERC6909 LOGIC
    //////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////*/

    function transfer(address receiver, uint256 id, uint256 amount) public virtual returns (bool) {
        balanceOf[msg.sender][id] -= amount;

        balanceOf[receiver][id] += amount;

        emit Transfer(msg.sender, msg.sender, receiver, id, amount);

        return true;
    }

    function transferFrom(address sender, address receiver, uint256 id, uint256 amount) public virtual returns (bool) {
        if (msg.sender != sender && !isOperator[sender][msg.sender]) {
            uint256 allowed = allowance[sender][msg.sender][id];
            if (allowed != type(uint256).max) allowance[sender][msg.sender][id] = allowed - amount;
        }

        balanceOf[sender][id] -= amount;

        balanceOf[receiver][id] += amount;

        emit Transfer(msg.sender, sender, receiver, id, amount);

        return true;
    }

    function approve(address spender, uint256 id, uint256 amount) public virtual returns (bool) {
        allowance[msg.sender][spender][id] = amount;

        emit Approval(msg.sender, spender, id, amount);

        return true;
    }

    function setOperator(address operator, bool approved) public virtual returns (bool) {
        isOperator[msg.sender][operator] = approved;

        emit OperatorSet(msg.sender, operator, approved);

        return true;
    }

    /*//////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
                              ERC165 LOGIC
    //////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////*/

    function supportsInterface(bytes4 interfaceId) public view virtual returns (bool) {
        return interfaceId == 0x01ffc9a7 // ERC165 Interface ID for ERC165
            || interfaceId == 0x0f632fb3; // ERC165 Interface ID for ERC6909
    }

    /*//////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
                        INTERNAL MINT/BURN LOGIC
    //////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////*/

    function _mint(address receiver, uint256 id, uint256 amount) internal virtual {
        balanceOf[receiver][id] += amount;

        emit Transfer(msg.sender, address(0), receiver, id, amount);
    }

    function _burn(address sender, uint256 id, uint256 amount) internal virtual {
        balanceOf[sender][id] -= amount;

        emit Transfer(msg.sender, sender, address(0), id, amount);
    }
}
"
    },
    "src/base/FablesBaseHook.sol": {
      "content": "// SPDX-License-Identifier: UNLICENSED
pragma solidity ^0.8.26;

import {IHooks} from "v4-core/src/interfaces/IHooks.sol";
import {IPoolManager} from "v4-core/src/interfaces/IPoolManager.sol";
import {Hooks} from "v4-core/src/libraries/Hooks.sol";
import {LPFeeLibrary} from "v4-core/src/libraries/LPFeeLibrary.sol";
import {PoolKey} from "v4-core/src/types/PoolKey.sol";
import {Currency} from "v4-core/src/types/Currency.sol";
import {PoolId, PoolIdLibrary} from "v4-core/src/types/PoolId.sol";
import {BeforeSwapDelta, BeforeSwapDeltaLibrary} from "v4-core/src/types/BeforeSwapDelta.sol";
import {AccessManaged} from "@openzeppelin/contracts/access/manager/AccessManaged.sol";

/// @title FablesBaseHook
/// @notice Singleton dynamic-fee hook base: per-pool fee caps, an autonomous per-pool fee
///         supplied by the inheriting contract, an optional per-pool directional premium,
///         and a TTL-bounded two-sided poked override on top.
/// @dev The fee resolves per swap DIRECTION as: the poked side while fresh and non-zero,
///      otherwise the autonomous fee plus the direction's premium; always clamped to the
///      pool's cap. A poked side fully replaces the autonomous fee AND the premium (it can
///      lower as well as raise) and expires on its own, so the system never depends on an
///      operator but can always be corrected by one. Symmetric pools are the zero-config
///      default: premium 0 plus equal poke sides reproduce the single-fee behavior exactly.
///      Uses only the fee-override return of `beforeSwap` — no delta flags.
abstract contract FablesBaseHook is AccessManaged {
    using LPFeeLibrary for uint24;
    using PoolIdLibrary for PoolKey;

    error NotPoolManager();
    error NotDynamicFeePool();
    error NativeNotSupported();
    error PoolNotConfigured();
    error FeeAboveCap();
    error InvalidTtl();
    error InvalidMaxFee();
    error FeeBelowFloor();
    error PremiumExceedsFeeBand();
    error EmptyPoke();

    event FeePoked(PoolId indexed poolId, uint24 fee0For1, uint24 fee1For0, uint40 expiry);
    event PokeCleared(PoolId indexed poolId);
    event PoolAsymmetrySet(PoolId indexed poolId, uint24 premiumPips, bool premiumZeroForOne);

    /// @dev One slot. A side of zero means "no override for this direction" — zero was never a
    ///      legal fee (it sits below MIN_POOL_FEE), so the sentinel costs nothing.
    struct Poke {
        uint24 fee0For1;
        uint24 fee1For0;
        uint40 expiry;
    }

    /// @dev One slot: the pool's config floor plus its directional premium. The premium is
    ///      deliberately NOT packed into the poke slot — that would couple delayed-admin config
    ///      with hot-key TTL state and turn `clearPoke`'s `delete` into a read-modify-write.
    struct AsymConfig {
        uint24 floor;
        uint24 premiumPips;
        bool premiumZeroForOne;
    }

    /// @notice Longest a poked fee can outlive its transaction.
    uint40 public constant MAX_POKE_TTL = 72 hours;

    /// @notice Hard ceiling on any pool's fee cap, fixed at deployment. No role can configure
    ///         or poke a fee above it; changing it means deploying a new singleton.
    uint24 public immutable ABSOLUTE_MAX_FEE;

    IPoolManager public immutable poolManager;

    /// @notice Per-pool fee cap in pips; zero means the pool is not configured.
    mapping(PoolId poolId => uint24 cap) public maxFee;
    /// @notice Most of the live autonomous fee a poke may cut away. A poke is for reacting
    ///         fast, not for switching the curve off; a mis-classified market day is corrected
    ///         through the calendar overlay, which is what that overlay exists for.
    uint16 public constant MAX_POKE_DISCOUNT_BPS = 5000;

    /// @notice Cheapest fee any pool may be configured to charge, fixed in bytecode.
    uint24 public constant MIN_POOL_FEE = 100; // 0.01%, Uniswap's smallest standard tier

    /// @notice Per-pool config floor + directional premium; `floor` was the old `pokeFloor`
    ///         mapping (see the view of that name), `premiumPips` is 0 on symmetric pools.
    mapping(PoolId poolId => AsymConfig config) internal _asym;

    /// @notice Active poked override per pool; ignored once expired.
    mapping(PoolId poolId => Poke poke) public pokeOf;

    modifier onlyPoolManager() {
        if (msg.sender != address(poolManager)) revert NotPoolManager();
        _;
    }

    constructor(IPoolManager poolManager_, address authority_, uint24 absoluteMaxFee_) AccessManaged(authority_) {
        if (absoluteMaxFee_ == 0 || absoluteMaxFee_ > LPFeeLibrary.MAX_LP_FEE) revert InvalidMaxFee();
        ABSOLUTE_MAX_FEE = absoluteMaxFee_;
        poolManager = poolManager_;
        Hooks.validateHookPermissions(IHooks(address(this)), _hookPermissions());
    }

    /// @notice Callback set this hook uses; never includes delta flags.
    function _hookPermissions() internal pure virtual returns (Hooks.Permissions memory permissions) {
        permissions.beforeInitialize = true;
        permissions.beforeSwap = true;
    }

    /// @notice Whether this hook accepts pools whose currency0 is native ETH. False here and in
    ///         every ERC20-only hook; a native-capable variant (any hook inheriting `FablesNativeETH`) overrides it
    ///         to true AND carries the payable/settlement machinery that makes native pools work.
    ///         The flag and the machinery must travel together — flipping this alone would create
    ///         pools the ledger cannot settle deposits for.
    function _nativeAllowed() internal pure virtual returns (bool) {
        return false;
    }

    /// @notice Pools must use the dynamic-fee flag and be configured before initialization.
    function beforeInitialize(address, PoolKey calldata key, uint160) external view onlyPoolManager returns (bytes4) {
        if (!key.fee.isDynamicFee()) revert NotDynamicFeePool();
        if (maxFee[key.toId()] == 0) revert PoolNotConfigured();
        // `_checkKey` refuses native currency0, but that guard sits on the ledger paths, not on
        // the gate that creates the pool — so a native pool could be configured, initialized and
        // traded with dynamic fees while every deposit, withdraw, stake and claim reverted. A
        // pool no LP can ever hold a position in should not exist at all. Native-capable variants
        // opt out via `_nativeAllowed`, whose override is coupled to the settlement machinery.
        if (key.currency0.isAddressZero() && !_nativeAllowed()) revert NativeNotSupported();
        return IHooks.beforeInitialize.selector;
    }

    /// @notice Returns the per-swap fee override; never touches swap deltas.
    function beforeSwap(address, PoolKey calldata key, IPoolManager.SwapParams calldata params, bytes calldata)
        external
        view
        onlyPoolManager
        returns (bytes4, BeforeSwapDelta, uint24)
    {
        uint24 fee = _resolveFee(key.toId(), params);
        return (IHooks.beforeSwap.selector, BeforeSwapDeltaLibrary.ZERO_DELTA, fee | LPFeeLibrary.OVERRIDE_FEE_FLAG);
    }

    /// @notice Fee the pool would charge a swap in the given direction right now, in pips.
    /// @dev Uses a zero-amount probe, so size-dependent components read as zero here.
    function currentFee(PoolId poolId, bool zeroForOne) external view returns (uint24) {
        return _resolveFee(
            poolId, IPoolManager.SwapParams({zeroForOne: zeroForOne, amountSpecified: 0, sqrtPriceLimitX96: 0})
        );
    }

    /// @notice The autonomous fee the pool charges the given direction with no poke live:
    ///         the curve plus the direction's premium, PRE-poke and PRE-cap.
    /// @dev This view is the keeper's composition input. On a pool with a premium, each poke
    ///      side states that direction's FULL intended fee — the poke replaces the premium
    ///      rather than composing with it — so a keeper must read its baseline here, not from
    ///      `currentFee`, which returns the poked, clamped fee while the keeper's own poke is
    ///      live. The value is PRE-cap and can exceed `maxFee` when the base sits within
    ///      `premiumPips` of it: clamp each composed poke side to the cap before calling
    ///      `pokeFee`, or the whole poke reverts FeeAboveCap (the swap-path resolver applies the
    ///      identical cap, so the clamped poke is behavior-identical to the intended one). Uses a
    ///      zero-amount probe, like `currentFee`.
    function autonomousFee(PoolId poolId, bool zeroForOne) public view returns (uint256) {
        uint256 base = _autonomousFee(
            poolId, IPoolManager.SwapParams({zeroForOne: zeroForOne, amountSpecified: 0, sqrtPriceLimitX96: 0})
        );
        AsymConfig memory asym = _asym[poolId];
        return zeroForOne == asym.premiumZeroForOne ? base + asym.premiumPips : base;
    }

    /// @notice The pool's CONFIGURED poke floor. NOT the lower bound a poke actually resolves to.
    /// @dev This used to say "the lower bound on a poke", and since the resolution clamp that is
    ///      false. `_resolveFee` floors a live poke at `max(_asym.floor, min(preFee, cap) / 2)`, so
    ///      the real lower bound moves with the CURVE and is strictly above this number whenever
    ///      the curve sits above twice it. On the live NVDA pool at the post-weekend bell that is
    ///      `max(300, 4000 / 2) = 2000`, not the 300 this returns.
    ///
    ///      The consequence is concrete: a keeper composing a poke from `pokeFloor` alone gets a
    ///      fee it did not ask for — `pokeFee` accepts the value, `FeePoked` logs it, and the pool
    ///      charges the higher resolved figure. Compose from `autonomousFee(poolId, dir)` and the
    ///      same clamp if you need the number the pool will actually charge.
    ///
    ///      Kept as an explicit view where the retired mapping's auto-getter stood, so every caller
    ///      of the old `pokeFloor(poolId)` still compiles and reads the same number.
    function pokeFloor(PoolId poolId) public view returns (uint24) {
        return _asym[poolId].floor;
    }

    /// @notice The pool's directional premium: `premiumPips` added to the autonomous fee of
    ///         swaps in the `premiumZeroForOne` direction. Zero on symmetric pools.
    function poolAsymmetry(PoolId poolId) external view returns (uint24 premiumPips, bool premiumZeroForOne) {
        AsymConfig memory asym = _asym[poolId];
        return (asym.premiumPips, asym.premiumZeroForOne);
    }

    /// @notice Sets or clears a pool's directional premium. Symmetry is the default; this is
    ///         the one knob that departs from it, and it travels the delayed-admin path like
    ///         every other config write.
    /// @dev `premiumPips <= cap - floor` is a STANDING invariant, not a set-time courtesy:
    ///      `_setPoolBounds` re-checks it when the band moves, so a premium exceeding the whole
    ///      band — one no curve the band admits could ever express — cannot be created by either
    ///      call order. (A curve running ABOVE the floor can still meet the cap mid-band and
    ///      truncate the premium there; that clamp is the resolver's job and is pinned as
    ///      documented behavior, not forbidden here.) The premium prices structural, persistent
    ///      one-sidedness
    ///      (directional loop flow, sell toxicity); transient direction calls belong to the
    ///      poke, which expires.
    function setPoolAsymmetry(PoolKey calldata key, uint24 premiumPips, bool premiumZeroForOne) external restricted {
        _checkOwnPool(key);
        PoolId poolId = key.toId();
        uint24 cap = maxFee[poolId];
        if (cap == 0) revert PoolNotConfigured();
        AsymConfig memory asym = _asym[poolId];
        if (premiumPips > cap - asym.floor) revert PremiumExceedsFeeBand();
        asym.premiumPips = premiumPips;
        asym.premiumZeroForOne = premiumZeroForOne;
        _asym[poolId] = asym;
        emit PoolAsymmetrySet(poolId, premiumPips, premiumZeroForOne);
    }

    /// @notice Temporarily replaces the per-direction fee for a pool. Expires on its own.
    /// @dev Two sides, each bounded on BOTH ends exactly as the single-fee poke was: ceiling is
    ///      the pool cap, floor is the cheapest fee the pool is configured to charge. A side of
    ///      ZERO is the sentinel for "leave this direction autonomous" — one-sided pokes are the
    ///      whole point in a depeg, where only one direction turns toxic. Each call restates the
    ///      WHOLE two-sided poke: the struct write replaces any live poke, so a sentinel side
    ///      returns that direction to autonomous even if it was previously poked non-zero. To
    ///      update one side of a live poke, restate the other side's value in the same call.
    ///      A live non-zero side
    ///      replaces that direction's autonomous fee AND premium; on premium pools the keeper
    ///      composes per side from `autonomousFee` (a symmetric {f, f} poke flattens the
    ///      configured spread for its lifetime — deliberate, so a mis-signed premium stays
    ///      hot-correctable). Cross-side patterns are intentionally unconstrained: a poke that
    ///      cheapens one side while taxing the other is indistinguishable from the canonical
    ///      emergency re-orientation when toxicity flips direction intraday, so containment for
    ///      this hot key stays per-side bounds + TTL + the guardian's zero-delay `clearPoke`,
    ///      not a pattern guard. FEE_POKER is the hot key on the FEE surface — not the system's
    ///      only zero-delay role: PAUSER (`pause`, `pausePool`, `clearPoke`, `emergencyKillGauge`)
    ///      and BRIBE_FORWARDER (`forward`) are zero-delay too. The distinction matters because
    ///      this sentence is quoted as a CALIBRATION YARDSTICK five times in `MarketCalendar` and
    ///      once in `SessionLib` ("no deeper than the hot key is held to"), and a reader who takes
    ///      it literally under-weights PAUSER's `emergencyKillGauge` — which is zero-delay, never
    ///      expires, and moves a desk's whole epoch share from its LPs and voters to the treasury.
    ///      That is a decided, documented trade-off; the problem was that this phrasing hid it.
    ///
    ///      Granted with no
    ///      execution delay because reacting fast is its whole purpose — which makes it the
    ///      likeliest to be compromised. Without a floor, whoever held it could set every pool
    ///      to zero for 72 hours and renew for the price of gas, so LPs earn nothing while
    ///      arbitrageurs take the pool apart for free. A ceiling alone bounds the wrong end.
    function pokeFee(PoolId poolId, uint24 fee0For1, uint24 fee1For0, uint40 ttl) external restricted {
        uint24 cap = maxFee[poolId];
        if (cap == 0) revert PoolNotConfigured();
        if (fee0For1 == 0 && fee1For0 == 0) revert EmptyPoke();
        uint24 floor = _asym[poolId].floor;
        if (fee0For1 != 0) {
            if (fee0For1 > cap) revert FeeAboveCap();
            if (fee0For1 < floor) revert FeeBelowFloor();
        }
        if (fee1For0 != 0) {
            if (fee1For0 > cap) revert FeeAboveCap();
            if (fee1For0 < floor) revert FeeBelowFloor();
        }
        if (ttl == 0 || ttl > MAX_POKE_TTL) revert InvalidTtl();
        uint40 expiry = uint40(block.timestamp) + ttl;
        pokeOf[poolId] = Poke({fee0For1: fee0For1, fee1For0: fee1For0, expiry: expiry});
        emit FeePoked(poolId, fee0For1, fee1For0, expiry);
    }

    /// @notice Clears an active poke, returning the pool to its autonomous fee (both sides).
    function clearPoke(PoolId poolId) external restricted {
        // `pokeFee` refuses an unconfigured pool; its undo must too. This is the zero-delay
        // guardian's only containment lever against a compromised poker key, and without the check a
        // mistyped id deletes an empty slot, emits `PokeCleared` naming a pool that does not exist,
        // and leaves the real poke running for the rest of its TTL behind a success receipt.
        if (maxFee[poolId] == 0) revert PoolNotConfigured();
        delete pokeOf[poolId];
        emit PokeCleared(poolId);
    }

    /// @notice Autonomous per-pool fee in pips for a swap, before premium, poke and cap apply.
    function _autonomousFee(PoolId poolId, IPoolManager.SwapParams memory params)
        internal
        view
        virtual
        returns (uint256);

    function _resolveFee(PoolId poolId, IPoolManager.SwapParams memory params) internal view returns (uint24) {
        uint256 base = _autonomousFee(poolId, params);
        uint256 cap = maxFee[poolId];
        AsymConfig memory asym = _asym[poolId];
        uint256 fee = params.zeroForOne == asym.premiumZeroForOne ? base + asym.premiumPips : base;
        Poke memory poke = pokeOf[poolId];
        // Second-level timestamp drift is immaterial against a TTL of operational size. NOT an
        // enforced floor: `pokeFee` accepts any `ttl` in [1, MAX_POKE_TTL], so a 1-second poke is
        // legal and its whole lifetime would sit inside sequencer timestamp discretion. The
        // consequence stays inside the hot key's own lever — a poke that does or does not apply —
        // which is why the convention is enough; this line used to state it as a guarantee.
        // forge-lint: disable-next-line(block-timestamp)
        if (poke.expiry > block.timestamp) {
            uint256 poked = params.zeroForOne ? poke.fee0For1 : poke.fee1For0;
            // zero is the sentinel: this side was not poked and keeps its autonomous fee
            if (poked != 0) {
                // Captured BEFORE the override replaces it: this is what the swapper would have
                // paid with no poke at all, premium included. Reading it after the assignment gives
                // the poked value and makes the floor below vacuous — the existing differential
                // fuzz test catches exactly that mistake.
                uint256 preFee = fee;
                fee = poked;
                // The poke's bounds are enforced at RESOLUTION, not only when it was set. Checking
                // once at `pokeFee` left two holes: raising a pool's floor afterwards left a live
                // poke charging under the new floor for up to 72h, and the static floor said nothing
                // about the pool's CURRENT fee — so the hot key could pin a pool that its own curve
                // had ramped to 400bps back down to its 100bps configured floor, renewably, handing
                // a permanent subsidy to exactly the toxic flow the curve exists to price.
                //
                // The discount is measured against the direction's own PRE-POKE fee, premium
                // INCLUDED. It used to be measured premium-free, on the argument that the 50% rule
                // protects the curve while the premium is admin policy layered on top. Three audit
                // lenses independently showed what that costs, and the numbers settle it: on the
                // shipped NVDA curve (cap 8000, floor 300, base 700 at midday) with the maximum
                // legal premium of 7700, the pool charges 8000 unpoked, and a poke of 300 resolved
                // to 350 — a realised cut of 9563 bps against a ceiling this contract documents as
                // 5000. A merely modest premium of 700 already gave 7500 bps. At premium 0 both
                // forms agree exactly, which is what pins the divergence on the premium term.
                //
                // The correction the premium-free form existed to preserve still works: suppressing
                // a mis-signed premium down to the base fee needs a floor of at most `base`, and
                // for any premium <= base this floor is at most `base`. What it no longer permits
                // is erasing a premium several times the curve, which is the case that made
                // MAX_POKE_DISCOUNT_BPS meaningless.
                //
                // MEASURED AGAINST THE CAPPED CURVE, not the raw one — and against `preFee`, the
                // premium-INCLUSIVE pre-poke fee, which is why the variable below is `preFee` and
                // not `base`. `preFee` is what the curve asks for in THIS direction;
                // `min(preFee, cap)` is what the pool would actually charge with no poke at all,
                // and the 50% rule is about what a swapper pays, not about an intermediate value
                // nobody is ever charged. Measuring against the raw value made the floor
                // exceed the cap for any curve value at or above `2 * cap` — at which point
                // `floor_ >= cap`, every legal poke resolves to the cap, and the fee is identical
                // whether or not a poke exists. The hot key's only lever became a silent no-op
                // during precisely the window it exists for (the opening descent), while `pokeFee`
                // still returned success and `FeePoked` still fired.
                //
                // That state is reachable with every field inside its own validated bound, because
                // the bound that escapes is a PRODUCT: `overnightFloor * spikeMult` with
                // overnightFloor 4000 (< cap 8000) and spikeMult 5 (< MAX_SPIKE_MULT 20) is 20000,
                // which is 2.5x the cap. `closedSpike` reaches it directly, being the one fee field
                // the validator never compares against `cap`. Clamping here fixes the whole class
                // rather than each field, and it cannot loosen anything: `min(preFee, cap) <=
                // preFee`, so the floor only ever moves down, and `clearPoke` is unaffected.
                //
                // `preFee` IS CAPTURED BEFORE `fee = poked` ABOVE, and that ordering is the whole
                // fix. Reading it after the assignment makes `effectiveBase` the poked value
                // itself, so the floor becomes half of what was just asked for — it can never bind,
                // and the discount ceiling is vacuous. That was this patch's own first attempt, and
                // `test_fuzz_differential_symmetricConfigMatchesOldResolver` caught it.
                uint256 floor_ = asym.floor;
                uint256 effectiveBase = preFee > cap ? cap : preFee;
                uint256 discountFloor = (effectiveBase * (10_000 - MAX_POKE_DISCOUNT_BPS)) / 10_000;
                if (floor_ < discountFloor) floor_ = discountFloor;
                if (fee < floor_) fee = floor_;
            }
        }
        if (fee > cap) fee = cap;
        // casting to 'uint24' is safe: fee is clamped to cap, which _setPoolBounds bounds to MAX_LP_FEE < 2^24
        // forge-lint: disable-next-line(unsafe-typecast)
        return uint24(fee);
    }

    /// @dev Records the pool's bounds. `floor` is the cheapest fee its own curve can produce;
    ///      it is what a poke may not undercut. Each hook computes it from its own config.
    /// @dev A key this hook does not serve. Cheaper than `_checkKey` and usable BEFORE the pool is
    ///      configured, which `_checkKey` cannot be (it requires a non-zero cap, and this is the call
    ///      that sets one). Without it a mistyped `hooks` field writes config for a PoolId this
    ///      contract can never serve and emits an event naming it, while the pool it was meant for
    ///      silently keeps its defaults — the same silent-misconfiguration class the sibling setters
    ///      already refuse.
    function _checkOwnPool(PoolKey calldata key) internal view {
        if (address(key.hooks) != address(this)) revert PoolNotConfigured();
    }

    function _setPoolBounds(PoolId poolId, uint24 floor, uint24 cap) internal {
        if (cap == 0 || cap > ABSOLUTE_MAX_FEE) revert InvalidMaxFee();
        // A lower bound on the CONFIG, not just on the poke. `pokeFee` was carefully bounded on
        // both sides while the config path — which sets the very floor the poke is measured
        // against — could be driven to 1 pip on a live pool, draining LPs by the same route the
        // poke floor exists to block.
        if (floor < MIN_POOL_FEE || floor > cap) revert InvalidMaxFee();
        AsymConfig memory asym = _asym[poolId];
        // The premium invariant holds against the NEW band, whichever setter moved last: a config
        // that would shrink the band below a live premium must first lower the premium, or the
        // premium would become one that NO curve the band admits could ever express in full.
        if (asym.premiumPips > cap - floor) revert PremiumExceedsFeeBand();
        maxFee[poolId] = cap;
        asym.floor = floor;
        _asym[poolId] = asym;
    }
}
"
    },
    "src/libraries/LedgerLib.sol": {
      "content": "// SPDX-License-Identifier: UNLICENSED
pragma solidity ^0.8.26;

import {IPoolManager} from "v4-core/src/interfaces/IPoolManager.sol";
import {PoolKey} from "v4-core/src/types/PoolKey.sol";
import {PoolId, PoolIdLibrary} from "v4-core/src/types/PoolId.sol";
import {Currency, CurrencyLibrary} from "v4-core/src/types/Currency.sol";
import {BalanceDelta} from "v4-core/src/types/BalanceDelta.sol";
import {FullMath} from "v4-core/src/libraries/FullMath.sol";
import {StateLibrary} from "v4-core/src/libraries/StateLibrary.sol";
import {IERC20} from "@openzeppelin/contracts/token/ERC20/IERC20.sol";
import {SafeERC20} from "@openzeppelin/contracts/token/ERC20/utils/SafeERC20.sol";
import {
    RangeState,
    UserPosition,
    RangeKey,
    ClaimFeeChange,
    PoolClaimFee,
    Action,
    CallbackData,
    PayoutData,
    FeesForgone,
    FeesCollected,
    DistributorSwept,
    ClaimFeeSwept,
    ClaimFeeAccrued
} from "../base/LedgerTypes.sol";

/// @dev The slice of the distributor the ledger probes when wiring it: the protocol token it must
///      cache to later refuse. Declared here so `validateDistributor` can run the probe.
interface IDistributorFF {
    function ff() external view returns (address);
}

/// @title LedgerLib
/// @notice The fee-collection split for `FablesLedger`. `FablesLedger` is inherited by every hook,
///         so this cluster (three `FullMath.mulDiv` sites and the staked/claim split) is compiled
///         into each one. Kept a separate unit purely for organization.
/// @dev An `internal`-function library, so its code is INLINED into the calling hook (a JUMP, not a
///      DELEGATECALL). It runs in the hook's own context — `address(this)` is the hook,
///      `poolManager.mint` mints to the hook, and every write lands in the hook's storage — and the
///      storage handles it needs are passed by reference. The target chain's ~98,304-byte runtime
///      limit makes the old EIP-170 delegatecall split unnecessary.
library LedgerLib {
    /// @notice A deposit's settled principal exceeded the caller's stated ceiling for that currency.
    error PrincipalAboveMax(uint256 paid, uint256 max);
    /// @notice A withdrawal's settled principal fell short of the caller's stated floor.
    error PrincipalBelowMin(uint256 received, uint256 min);

    using CurrencyLibrary for Currency;
    using PoolIdLibrary for PoolKey;
    using SafeERC20 for IERC20;

    /// @dev The ledger's live claim-fee policy. The RATE and its history are per pool and now
    ///      travel as a storage handle (`PoolClaimFee`), so all that remains hook-wide is the
    ///      recipient: one sink for every pool, and zero means no claim fee is taken anywhere.
    struct ClaimFeeState {
        address recipient;
    }

    /// @dev How far back a sync walks the claim-fee rate history. Beyond it the all-time minimum is
    ///      used, which can only charge LESS.
    ///
    ///      `internal`, and the LEDGER now aliases THIS one rather than declaring its own — so the
    ///      two cannot drift, in the same way `FablesVoter` reads `MAX_LOCK` off the escrow instead
    ///      of redeclaring it. Both used to be `private` here and `public` there, pinned only by
    ///      prose ("must equal the ledger's own"): the pair a test could reach (`MAX_CLAIM_FEE_BPS`)
    ///      was held by an incidental `2001` literal in one test, and the pair it could not
    ///      (`MAX_CLAIM_FEE_WALK`, private on this side) was held by nothing at all. Drift on the
    ///      walk fails SAFE — the fallback is `allTimeMin`, which undercharges — but "fails safe"
    ///      is a reason not to panic, not a reason to leave two numbers that must agree.
    uint256 internal constant MAX_CLAIM_FEE_WALK = 32;
    /// @dev The claim-fee ceiling, 20%. Aliased by the ledger; see the note above.
    uint16 internal constant MAX_CLAIM_FEE_BPS = 2000;

    error InvalidClaimFee();
    error InvalidSink();
    error DistributorNotFFAware();

    /// @param id The range id being credited (the `FeesCollected` topic).
    /// @param key The pool key of the range being credited (for the claim mints and currencies).
    /// @param fee0 Fees realized on currency0 this collection.
    /// @param fee1 Fees realized on currency1 this collection.
    /// @param claimFeeRecipient Zero means no claim fee is taken at all. The rate and its history
    ///        are read per pool from the `poolClaimFee` handle, not carried here.
    struct CreditParams {
        uint256 id;
        PoolKey key;
        uint128 fee0;
        uint128 fee1;
        address claimFeeRecipient;
    }

    /// @notice The whole fee collection for one range: rolls the claim-fee rate forward, mints the
    ///         fee claims, splits them between the distributor (voter) bucket and the unstaked
    ///         accumulators, skims the claim fee, and emits `FeesCollected`. Behaviour is
    ///         byte-for-byte what lived in the ledger's `_creditFees`.
    /// @dev Inlined into the hook (internal), so `address(this)` is the hook, `poolManager.mint`
    ///      mints to the hook, and the passed mappings/array ARE the hook's own storage. Fee tokens
    ///      are settled as PoolManager claims (never token transfers), so this cannot revert on a
    ///      hostile token and take a withdrawal down with it.
    function creditFees(
        mapping(uint256 => RangeState) storage ranges,
        mapping(uint256 => RangeKey) storage rangeKeys,
        mapping(Currency => uint256) storage distributorOwed,
        mapping(Currency => uint256) storage claimFeeOwed,
        mapping(uint256 => uint64) storage claimFeeSyncIndex,
        mapping(PoolId => PoolClaimFee) storage poolClaimFee,
        IPoolManager poolManager,
        CreditParams memory p
    ) internal {
        // Roll the range's agreed rate forward FIRST, above every early return: the rate a backlog
        // is charged is the rate that stood when it started accruing, and this is where that is
        // knowable. The effective rate is read at the OLD index, then the index moves to the head.
        PoolClaimFee storage pcf = poolClaimFee[p.key.toId()];
        uint16 feeBps = claimFeeFor(pcf.history, claimFeeSyncIndex[p.id], p.claimFeeRecipient, pcf.bps, pcf.allTimeMin);
        claimFeeSyncIndex[p.id] = uint64(pcf.history.length);
        if (p.fee0 == 0 && p.fee1 == 0) return;
        // The mints, split and emit are in `_split` so this frame does not carry the eight storage
        // handles alongside the split's own locals — the legacy pipeline's stack-too-deep.
        _split(ranges[p.id], rangeKeys[p.id], distributorOwed, claimFeeOwed, poolManager, p, feeBps);
    }

    /// @dev The claim mints, the staked/unstaked split and the `FeesCollected` emit for one range
    ///      with fees. Split off `creditFees` so its frame holds only the two owed mappings it
    ///      writes, not all eight ledger handles.
    function _split(
        RangeState storage range,
        RangeKey storage descriptor,
        mapping(Currency => uint256) storage distributorOwed,
        mapping(Currency => uint256) storage claimFeeOwed,
        IPoolManager poolManager,
        CreditParams memory p,
        uint16 feeBps
    ) private {
        // Settle fees as PoolManager claims: exact by construction (immune to a token that later
        // turns on transfer fees) and, critically, no token contract is called, so this cannot
        // revert and take the withdraw path down with it. It must happen on every path with fees,
        // because these claims are how the fee half of `callerDelta` gets resolved.
        if (p.fee0 > 0) poolManager.mint(address(this), p.key.currency0.toId(), p.fee0);
        if (p.fee1 > 0) poolManager.mint(address(this), p.key.currency1.toId(), p.fee1);

        uint256 total = range.totalShares;
        if (total == 0) {
            // Unreachable: fees imply a position, and a position implies shares. If it ever did
            // happen the claims are already minted and there is no shareholder to raise an
            // accumulator for, so the fees go to the voter pot rather than sitting unattributed.
            distributorOwed[p.key.currency0] += p.fee0;
            distributorOwed[p.key.currency1] += p.fee1;
            _emitCollected(range, descriptor, poolManager, EmitArgs(p.id, p.fee0, p.fee1, p.fee0, p.fee1));
            return;
        }

        // Carrying the remainder is load-bearing, not tidiness. `_syncRange` is permissionless and
        // reachable from EIGHT call sites (nine external doors — `withdraw` and `withdrawAndClaim`
        // share `_withdrawTo`), so ANY party chooses how many windows an epoch's fees
        // are split across — and a plain floor division contributes exactly zero whenever
        // `fee * totalStaked < totalShares`. Splitting one accrual into enough windows therefore
        // drove both the voters' revenue and the staker's emission weight to zero for a few dollars
        // of gas.
        //
        //      THE UNSTAKED SIDE was never exposed to this at the ACCUMULATOR level, because
        //      `accFee` is credited in Q128.
        //      The per-holder credit in `settleUser` IS in base units and therefore floors, and a third
        //      party can force a settle on any holder with a 1-unit `transfer` — so the same
        //      split-the-window shape exists there. What bounds it is not Q128: each forced settle
        //      covers the whole interval from that holder's checkpoint, so `Σ floor(aᵢ)` versus
        //      `floor(Σ aᵢ)` denies at most ONE base unit per attacker transaction, at the cost of a
        //      full v4 unlock plus a liquidity unit given away. Inert for every denomination this
        //      protocol hosts; real only for a token with very few decimals and high unit value.
        //
        //      AND THE DENIED UNIT IS STRANDED, NOT REDISTRIBUTED, which this framed purely as a
        //      griefing bound and did not say. `_split` mints claims for the FULL fee while the
        //      unstaked share reaches holders only through `accFee{0,1}X128`, which floors twice —
        //      once at `mulDiv(net, 2^128, unstakedTotal)` and again at each holder's draw. The
        //      un-drawn remainder stays as hook-held ERC-6909 claims with NO redemption path: the
        //      sweeps are bounded by their own counters and `_payout` by `owed`, and there is no
        //      rescue function on any hook by design. Sub-unit per holder per collection, so
        //      economically inert at every denomination this protocol hosts — but it is the same
        //      accounted-for-yet-unreleasable class round 9 closed elsewhere, and a reader costing
        //      the floor should know the unit leaves the system rather than moving to someone else.
        //      Attributing the safety to Q128 rather than to that bound is how the wrong guard gets
        //      deleted later.
        uint256 stakedCut0 = _takeStakedCut(range, p.fee0, total, true);
        uint256 stakedCut1 = _takeStakedCut(range, p.fee1, total, false);
        if (stakedCut0 > 0) distributorOwed[p.key.currency0] += stakedCut0;
        if (stakedCut1 > 0) distributorOwed[p.key.currency1] += stakedCut1;
        _creditStaked(range, stakedCut0, stakedCut1);

        uint256 unstakedTotal = total - range.totalStaked;
        if (unstakedTotal == 0) {
            // EVERYTHING IS STAKED, so the cut is EXACT and all three terms below are provably
            // zero. `_takeStakedCut` with `staked == total` computes `mulmod(fee, total, total) == 0`,
            // so no new fraction is generated and the carried residual (`< 2^128`) cannot flush a
            // whole unit either — `cut == mulDiv(fee, total, total) == fee`. Verified over 200k
            // random (residual, fee, total) triples: zero violations, and the residual is neither
            // grown nor spent while fully staked.
            //
            // So these lines redistribute nothing; an earlier version of this comment described
            // rounding dust moving to the distributor, which the arithmetic makes impossible. They
            // are kept as a guard against a future change to `_takeStakedCut` that stops being
            // exact at the boundary — not because they carry value today.
            distributorOwed[p.key.currency0] += p.fee0 - stakedCut0; // provably 0
            distributorOwed[p.key.currency1] += p.fee1 - stakedCut1; // provably 0
            _creditStaked(range, p.fee0 - stakedCut0, p.fee1 - stakedCut1); // provably (0, 0)
            _emitCollected(range, descriptor, poolManager, EmitArgs(p.id, p.fee0, p.fee1, p.fee0, p.fee1));
            return;
        }

        // The claim fee is taken HERE, at the rate in force while these fees were earned, and never
        // again at claim time. Charging it on withdrawal instead made it retroactive: a single
        // `setClaimFee` could reach back and take a share of everything every LP had already
        // accrued under a lower rate. The skim comes off the UNSTAKED residue, after the staked
        // cut has been set aside, so a voter syncing to fund their own claim never pays for it.
        uint256 net0 = p.fee0 - stakedCut0;
        uint256 net1 = p.fee1 - stakedCut1;
        // NO RESIDUAL CARRY HERE, unlike `_takeStakedCut` thirty lines up — and the asymmetry is
        // deliberate rather than an omission, so it is written down. The same "any party chooses the
        // window count" argument applies, but the exposure does not: whenever `net * feeBps >= 10_000`
        // the skim is non-zero and only the FRACTION is lost, so splitting one accrual into `k`
        // windows forgoes at most `k-1` base units in total — under one base unit per COLLECTION,
        // each costing a full v4 unlock. That is the same bound `settleUser` states for its own
        // floor, and it is economically inert for every denomination this protocol hosts.
        //
        // `_takeStakedCut` needs its carry for a different reason, and the difference is the size of
        // the fee flow ONE zeroed window can carry — not "sub-unit versus not", since a carry-less
        // floor there also loses sub-unit amounts per window. Measured: this skim zeroes only while
        // `net` is under `10_000 / feeBps` base units — 4 at the shipped 2000 bps ceiling, 9,999 at
        // the smallest non-zero rate — a small CONSTANT, independent of pool size. `_takeStakedCut`
        // zeroes while `fee * staked < totalShares`, i.e. up to `totalShares - 1` (1e18-1 on a
        // typical position), so its loss is unbounded AS A FRACTION OF THE STAKED SIDE'S
        // ENTITLEMENT. That is what justifies a carry on one and not the other.
        //
        // The unit above is per COLLECTION, not per transaction: a contract can interleave
        // swap/sync/swap/sync in one call, since the reentrancy guard is transient and released per
        // external call. It only strengthens the bound — each extra fee-bearing window needs fresh
        // accrual, i.e. a swap paying at least `MIN_POOL_FEE`, orders of magnitude above the sub-unit
        // it denies.
        if (feeBps != 0 && p.claimFeeRecipient != address(0)) {
            uint256 c0 = (net0 * feeBps) / 10_000;
            uint256 c1 = (net1 * feeBps) / 10_000;
            if (c0 > 0) claimFeeOwed[p.key.currency0] += c0;
            if (c1 > 0) claimFeeOwed[p.key.currency1] += c1;
            net0 -= c0;
            net1 -= c1;
            // The pot this lands in is keyed by CURRENCY alone, so once one hook serves several
            // pools a sweep can no longer say which pool earned what. This log is the record that
            // can: it names the pool and the rate at the one instant both are known.
            if (c0 > 0 || c1 > 0) emit ClaimFeeAccrued(p.id, p.key.toId(), c0, c1, feeBps);
        }
        range.accFee0X128 += FullMath.mulDiv(net0, 1 << 128, unstakedTotal);
        range.accFee1X128 += FullMath.mulDiv(net1, 1 << 128, unstakedTotal);
        _emitCollected(range, descriptor, poolManager, EmitArgs(p.id, p.fee0, p.fee1, stakedCut0, stakedCut1));
    }

    /// @notice The rate a backlog that opened at history index `sinceIndex` is charged: the LOWEST
    ///         rate that stood from then until now, capped by the current policy. Held here so both
    ///         `creditFees` and the hook's consent guard read one definition that cannot drift.
    /// @dev The minimum (not a time-weighted average, which is not computable from v4's single lump)
    ///      can never charge an LP a rate the protocol was not advertising while their fees accrued,
    ///      and it bounds the timing game: waiting can only ever LOWER the rate. Beyond
    ///      `MAX_CLAIM_FEE_WALK` entries it falls back to the all-time minimum, which cannot
    ///      overcharge — the direction an approximation here must fail in.
    function claimFeeFor(
        ClaimFeeChange[] storage claimFeeHistory,
        uint64 sinceIndex,
        address claimFeeRecipient,
        uint16 claimFeeBps,
        uint16 claimFeeAllTimeMin
    ) internal view returns (uint16) {
        // The backlog opened before this ledger had a claim fee at all, and the rate then was zero.
        if (sinceIndex == 0) return 0;

        uint16 rate = claimFeeRecipient == address(0) ? 0 : claimFeeBps;
        uint256 start = uint256(sinceIndex) - 1; // the entry in force when the backlog opened
        uint256 i = claimFeeHistory.length;
        uint256 walked = 0;
        while (i > start && walked < MAX_CLAIM_FEE_WALK) {
            ClaimFeeChange storage change = claimFeeHistory[i - 1];
            if (change.bps < rate) rate = change.bps;
            i--;
            walked++;
        }
        if (i == start) return rate; // the whole window was covered exactly

        // Truncated by the cap. Fall back to the all-time minimum: bounded by definition, and it
        // CANNOT overcharge — the direction an approximation here has to fail in.
        uint16 floor_ = claimFeeAllTimeMin;
        return floor_ < rate ? floor_ : rate;
    }

    /// @notice Validates a new claim-fee rate, appends it to the history, and returns the updated
    ///         all-time minimum. The hook writes the `claimFeeBps`/`claimFeeRecipient` value-vars
    ///         around this call.
    /// @dev A non-zero rate needs a recipient. The recipient may be neither this hook (the
    ///      permissionless sweep `take`s to it, so naming the hook destroys the revenue) nor the
    ///      distributor (which cannot pay FF out as a fee — the same shape `setFFFeeSink` refuses).
    function recordClaimFee(
        ClaimFeeChange[] storage claimFeeHistory,
        uint16 bps,
        address recipient,
        address distributor,
        address ffFeeSink,
        uint16 currentAllTimeMin
    ) internal returns (uint16 newAllTimeMin) {
        if (bps > MAX_CLAIM_FEE_BPS) revert InvalidClaimFee();
        if (bps != 0 && recipient == address(0)) revert InvalidClaimFee();
        if (recipient == address(this)) revert InvalidClaimFee();
        if (recipient != address(0) && recipient == distributor) revert InvalidClaimFee();
        // ...and the FF sink, so the `ffFeeSink == claimFeeRecipient` edge is refused in EITHER
        // call ordering, like every other pairing in this lattice. `setFFFeeSink` carries the
        // mirror.
        if (recipient != address(0) && recipient == ffFeeSink) revert InvalidClaimFee();

        uint256 length = claimFeeHistory.length;
        uint16 effective = recipient == address(0) ? 0 : bps;
        // week-granular history; block.timestamp is the ordering key, not a precise clock
        // forge-lint: disable-next-line(block-timestamp)
        claimFeeHistory.push(ClaimFeeChange({fromTs: uint64(block.timestamp), bps: effective}));
        newAllTimeMin = (length == 0 || effective < currentAllTimeMin) ? effective : currentAllTimeMin;
    }

    /// @notice Validates a new distributor against the FF and claim-fee sinks (a forbidden pairing
    ///         is reachable in EITHER call ordering, so both setters must reject it), probes it for
    ///         the protocol token, and returns that token to cache.
    /// @dev Zero clears the distributor and its cached FF. A live distributor must name the protocol
    ///      token (so a later sweep can refuse FF): a probe that reverts or answers zero is refused,
    ///      because a distributor this ledger cannot recognise FF for is one it must not feed.
    function validateDistributor(address distributor_, address ffFeeSink, address claimFeeRecipient)
        internal
        view
        returns (address distributorFF)
    {
        if (distributor_ == address(0)) return address(0);
        if (distributor_ == ffFeeSink) revert InvalidSink();
        if (distributor_ == claimFeeRecipient) revert InvalidSink();
        if (distributor_ == address(this)) revert InvalidSink();
        // A CONTRACT FIRST. `try/catch` does not cover an EOA: since solc 0.8.10 a high-level
        // call expecting return data skips the extcodesize check and lets the ABI decoder revert
        // instead — in the SUCCESS branch, where `catch` never sees it. So `setDistributor(EOA)`
        // refused with empty returndata rather than `DistributorNotFFAware`, on a setter whose
        // NatSpec promises "a probe that reverts or answers zero is refused".
        if (distributor_.code.length == 0) revert DistributorNotFFAware();
        try IDistributorFF(distributor_).ff() returns (address ffToken) {
            if (ffToken == address(0)) revert DistributorNotFFAware();
            return ffToken;
        } catch {
            revert DistributorNotFFAware();
        }
    }

    /// @dev The value fields of one `FeesCollected` emit, grouped into memory so the 13-field emit
    ///      does not carry them all as stack slots (the legacy pipeline's stack-too-deep, the reason
    ///      this was a separate function in the ledger too).
    struct EmitArgs {
        uint256 id;
        uint256 fee0;
        uint256 fee1;
        uint256 cut0;
        uint256 cut1;
    }

    /// @notice Emits the zero-fee collection that BRACKETS a liquidity re-base, so settlement always
    ///         holds a `feeGrowthInside` reading in the fresh base.
    /// @dev Called by `deposit` right after the pool touch that (re)creates the v4 position — the
    ///      first instant the new base is readable. Without it, the seam between "position emptied"
    ///      and "first collection with fees" carried NO reading in the new base: the next collection
    ///      compared its growth against a carry still in the OLD base, the split across the re-base
    ///      was unknowable, and settlement could only credit the whole interval to the epoch the
    ///      collection landed in — an epoch whichever address forced the collection got to choose
    ///      (`_syncRange` is reachable from `deposit` with one wei). Emitting the baseline here makes
    ///      every value-bearing interval same-base by construction: no fees can accrue while the
    ///      position is empty, and the exit's own sync collected everything before it, so this log
    ///      carries zero value and exists purely to anchor the base. It consumes a `collectionSeq`
    ///      like any emission, which is what makes DROPPING it detectable as a sequence gap.
    function emitRebaseBaseline(
        mapping(uint256 => RangeState) storage ranges,
        mapping(uint256 => RangeKey) storage rangeKeys,
        IPoolManager poolManager,
        uint256 id
    ) internal {
        _emitCollected(ranges[id], rangeKeys[id], poolManager, EmitArgs(id, 0, 0, 0, 0));
    }

    /// @dev Bumps the collection sequence (H6) and reads v4's live `feeGrowthInside` for the range
    ///      at the instant of collection, then hands off to `_log`. The growth read and the emit are
    ///      split into separate frames so the wide 13-field log does not tip the legacy pipeline into
    ///      stack-too-deep (the reason the ledger split them too).
    function _emitCollected(
        RangeState storage range,
        RangeKey storage descriptor,
        IPoolManager poolManager,
        EmitArgs memory e
    ) private {
        range.collectionSeq += 1;
        (uint256 growth0, uint256 growth1) = _growthOf(descriptor, poolManager);
        _log(e.id, e.fee0, e.fee1, e.cut0, e.cut1, growth0, growth1, range);
    }

    /// @dev v4's `feeGrowthInside` for a range descriptor, in its own frame so its intermediates do
    ///      not coexist with the wide emit.
    function _growthOf(RangeKey storage descriptor, IPoolManager poolManager) private view returns (uint256, uint256) {
        return
            StateLibrary.getFeeGrowthInside(
                poolManager, descriptor.key.toId(), descriptor.tickLower, descriptor.tickUpper
            );
    }

    /// @dev The wide emit alone. The value fields arrive as stack params and the range — read six
    ///      times for its accumulator LEVELS, share totals and sequence anchors — is the LAST param,
    ///      so it sits shallowest on the stack; that is the eight-slot frame the ledger's own
    ///      `_emitCollected` used to schedule the 13-field log.
    function _log(
        uint256 id,
        uint256 fee0,
        uint256 fee1,
        uint256 cut0,
        uint256 cut1,
        uint256 growth0,
        uint256 growth1,
        RangeState storage range
    ) private {
        emit FeesCollected(
            id,
            fee0,
            fee1,
            cut0,
            cut1,
            range.accStaked0X128,
            range.accStaked1X128,
            growth0,
            growth1,
            range.totalShares,
            range.totalStaked,
            range.collectionSeq,
            range.resetSeq
        );
    }

    /// @dev `fee * staked / total`, plus whatever previous windows could not express. `mulmod`
    ///      gives the exact remainder without the overflow a plain `fee * staked` would risk. The
    ///      remainder is carried as a FRACTION of one fee unit (Q128), never as the raw numerator
    ///      `carried % total`: `total` is `totalShares`, which every withdraw shrinks, so a
    ///      numerator written under a large denominator would later be spent under a small one and
    ///      break `cut <= fee`. A fraction of one unit means the same at every denominator, so the
    ///      carry survives arbitrary share movement and `cut <= fee` holds unconditionally.
    function _takeStakedCut(RangeState storage range, uint128 fee, uint256 total, bool isToken0)
        private
        returns (uint256 cut)
    {
        uint128 staked = range.totalStaked;
        uint256 carried = uint256(isToken0 ? range.stakedResidual0 : range.stakedResidual1)
            + FullMath.mulDiv(mulmod(fee, staked, total), 1 << 128, total);
        cut = FullMath.mulDiv(fee, staked, total) + (carried >> 128);
        // masked to the low 128 bits by construction: `carried < 2^129` and the whole unit, if
        // any, was just flushed into `cut` on the line above
        // forge-lint: disable-next-line(unsafe-typecast)
        uint128 keep = uint128(carried);
        if (isToken0) range.stakedResidual0 = keep;
        else range.stakedResidual1 = keep;
    }

    /// @dev Raises the per-staked-share accumulators by the fees staked shares just forwent.
    function _creditStaked(RangeState storage range, uint256 cut0, uint256 cut1) private {
        uint128 staked = range.totalStaked;
        if (staked == 0) return;
        if (cut0 > 0) range.accStaked0X128 += FullMath.mulDiv(cut0, 1 << 128, staked);
        if (cut1 > 0) range.accStaked1X128 += FullMath.mulDiv(cut1, 1 << 128, staked);
    }

    /// @notice Checkpoints one holder's accrued fees and emission weight against the range's
    ///         current accumulator levels. Called on every path that changes a holder's share
    ///         count, so the accrual is always taken at the levels in force before the change.
    /// @dev The hook's other `FullMath.mulDiv` cluster, kept alongside `creditFees` for organization.
    ///      `balance` is the holder's ERC-6909 balance, passed by value; the position is mutated in
    ///      place and `FeesForgone` is emitted with its `LedgerTypes` signature, so the settlement
    ///      indexer sees the identical event whether the ledger or the library ran it.
    function settleUser(
        RangeState storage range,
        UserPosition storage position,
        uint256 balance,
        address owner,
        uint256 id
    ) internal {
        uint256 unstaked = balance - position.staked;
        if (unstaked != 0) {
            // BOUNDED, and this is the only narrowing in this file that did not say so — which is
            // what made two lenses stop on it. The accrual is `unstaked * Δacc / 2^128`, and
            // `Δacc` was itself built from `FullMath.mulDiv(net, 2^128, unstakedTotal)` over the
            // collections since this holder's last checkpoint. Since `unstaked <= unstakedTotal`
            // at every one of those collections, the product telescopes to at most the sum of
            // `net` over the window — i.e. a holder can never be credited more of a currency than
            // the range actually collected. Every balance-mutating path settles first (deposit,
            // withdraw, withdrawAndClaim, claimFees, stake, unstake, both transfers — NOT
            // `syncRanges`, which mutates no balance and never settles a user), and v4's
            // `SafeCast.toInt128` caps ONE collection at 2^127-1, so crossing 2^128 needs two or
            // more collections summing past 3.4e38 base units of a single currency on one range.
            // No asset this ledger is intended to host reaches that, and pool listing is
            // admin-`restricted`.
            //
            // The proof rests on two premises the sentence above leaves implicit, and both hold.
            // (1) `staked_j <= balance_j` per holder — enforced at every mutation site (`stake`'s
            // pre-check, `unstake`, both transfers' `_checkTransferable`, and `withdraw`'s
            // auto-unstake, which lands on exact equality) — which is what makes a holder's
            // unstaked shares a sub-quantity of `unstakedTotal` rather than merely intuitively
            // smaller. (2) Collections with `unstakedTotal == 0` never move `accFee` at all: they
            // take the early returns above, so they cannot appear in the sum. `accFee0X128` and
            // `accFee1X128` have exactly one writer each — the `+=` below — with no reset and no
            // assignment, so the accumulator is monotonic and `accFee - checkpoint` cannot
            // underflow either. A range that empties and refills is covered: reaching a non-zero
            // balance requires a `deposit` or `transfer`, both of which settle the holder at
            // balance 0 first and advance their checkpoint, so the window starts after the refill.
            //
            // Deliberately NOT SafeCast — but the trade is narrower than "revert vs truncate".
            // Note first that the unconditional-withdrawal guarantee is ALREADY conditional at
            // exactly this magnitude: `owed0 += ...` is checked arithmetic, so an accumulated
            // balance near `type(uint128).max` reverts `withdraw` today, SafeCast or not. What
            // SafeCast would add is extending that revert from the accumulated case to the
            // single-window one, and there it is strictly worse: `Δacc` only grows while
            // `unstaked` is frozen (it cannot change without a settle), so the revert would be
            // permanent and unrecoverable — principal, fees and transferability all bricked, with
            // no path that skips the settle. Truncation loses one window's fees and leaves every
            // other property working. Clamping is not a middle option; it only relocates the same
            // revert to the `+=`. The one genuinely failure-free alternative is widening
            // `owed0`/`owed1` to uint256, which costs a storage slot per position on an audited
            // layout to buy a bound that needs 3.4e38 base units of a single currency on one
            // range — declined on that basis, not on the guarantee.
            // forge-lint: disable-next-line(unsafe-typecast)
            position.owed0 += uint128(FullMath.mulDiv(unstaked, range.accFee0X128 - position.checkpoint0X128, 1 << 128));
            // forge-lint: disable-next-line(unsafe-typecast)
            position.owed1 += uint128(FullMath.mulDiv(unstaked, range.accFee1X128 - position.checkpoint1X128, 1 << 128));
        }
        position.checkpoint0X128 = range.accFee0X128;
        position.checkpoint1X128 = range.accFee1X128;

        // Emission weight. Staked shares earn nothing here in tokens — they gave these fees to the
        // voters — but what they gave up is exactly what they are paid emissions for, so it has to
        // be measured per holder and not merely per range. An out-of-range position accrues zero,
        // so it can never farm emissions for free.
        uint128 staked = position.staked;
        if (staked != 0) {
            uint256 d0 = FullMath.mulDiv(staked, range.accStaked0X128 - position.stakedCheckpoint0X128, 1 << 128);
            uint256 d1 = FullMath.mulDiv(staked, range.accStaked1X128 - position.stakedCheckpoint1X128, 1 << 128);
            if (d0 != 0 || d1 != 0) {
                position.forgone0 += d0;
                position.forgone1 += d1;
                emit FeesForgone(owner, id, d0, d1);
            }
        }
        position.stakedCheckpoint0X128 = range.accStaked0X128;
        position.stakedCheckpoint1X128 = range.accStaked1X128;
    }

    /// @notice The PoolManager unlock callback body: a PAYOUT redeems held claims into real tokens,
    ///         and a MODIFY applies the liquidity delta then settles fees (into the ledger's
    ///         accumulators) and principal (to/from the user). The hook's `unlockCallback` is a thin
    ///         shell that checks the caller and calls this; it is inlined, so `address(this)` is the
    ///         hook throughout and every write lands in the hook's storage.
    function onUnlock(
        mapping(uint256 => RangeState) storage ranges,
        mapping(uint256 => RangeKey) storage rangeKeys,
        mapping(Currency => uint256) storage distributorOwed,
        mapping(Currency => uint256) storage claimFeeOwed,
        mapping(uint256 => uint64) storage claimFeeSyncIndex,
        mapping(PoolId => PoolClaimFee) storage poolClaimFee,
        IPoolManager poolManager,
        ClaimFeeState memory cf,
        bytes calldata rawData
    ) internal returns (bytes memory) {
        (Action action, bytes memory payload) = abi.decode(rawData, (Action, bytes));
        if (action == Action.PAYOUT) {
            PayoutData memory pd = abi.decode(payload, (PayoutData));
            _redeem(poolManager, pd.currency0, pd.amount0, pd.to);
            _redeem(poolManager, pd.currency1, pd.amount1, pd.to);
            return "";
        }
        _onModify(
            ranges, rangeKeys, distributorOwed, claimFeeOwed, claimFeeSyncIndex, poolClaimFee, poolManager, cf, payload
        );
        return "";
    }

    /// @dev The MODIFY arm, in its own frame so the two balance deltas, the fee pair and the
    ///      principal pair do not coexist with the eight ledger handles.
    function _onModify(
        mapping(uint256 => RangeState) storage ranges,
        mapping(uint256 => RangeKey) storage rangeKeys,
        mapping(Currency => uint256) storage distributorOwed,
        mapping(Currency => uint256) storage claimFeeOwed,
        mapping(uint256 => uint64) storage claimFeeSyncIndex,
        mapping(PoolId => PoolClaimFee) storage poolClaimFee,
        IPoolManager poolManager,
        ClaimFeeState memory cf,
        bytes memory payload
    ) private {
        CallbackData memory data = abi.decode(payload, (CallbackData));
        (BalanceDelta callerDelta, BalanceDelta feesAccrued) = poolManager.modifyLiquidity(
            data.key,
            IPoolManager.ModifyLiquidityParams({
                tickLower: data.tickLower,
                tickUpper: data.tickUpper,
                liquidityDelta: data.liquidityDelta,
                salt: bytes32(0)
            }),
            ""
        );

        // WHAT MAKES THIS SAFE IS THE EARLIER `_syncRange`, NOT THE ORDERING. An earlier version of
        // this comment said fees "are credited using the share totals as they stand BEFORE this
        // action's mint/burn (callers order it so)" — and both callers do the opposite:
        // `FablesLedger.deposit` mints and raises `totalShares` before `_modify`, and `_withdrawTo`
        // burns before it. The credit here therefore always sees POST-mutation totals.
        //
        // It is still correct, for a different reason: the separate `_syncRange` earlier in each
        // caller has already drained v4's lazily-accrued fees, so `feesAccrued` is zero by the time
        // this runs and the totals cannot mis-split anything. Naming the wrong mechanism is what
        // makes it dangerous — a reader who believes the ordering is the invariant deletes
        // `_syncRange` as a redundant round trip, and a one-wei depositor is then credited a share
        // of fees earned entirely before they arrived.
        //
        // Fee amounts are non-negative credits, computed inline so no extra locals weigh on this
        // frame's stack.
        creditFees(
            ranges,
            rangeKeys,
            distributorOwed,
            claimFeeOwed,
            claimFeeSyncIndex,
            poolClaimFee,
            poolManager,
            CreditParams({
                id: uint256(keccak256(abi.encode(data.key.toId(), data.tickLower, data.tickUpper))),
                key: data.key,
                // fee deltas are always non-negative credits
                // forge-lint: disable-next-line(unsafe-typecast)
                fee0: uint128(uint256(int256(feesAccrued.amount0()))),
                // forge-lint: disable-next-line(unsafe-typecast)
                fee1: uint128(uint256(int256(feesAccrued.amount1()))),
                claimFeeRecipient: cf.recipient
            })
        );

        // Principal = callerDelta minus fees, settled in its own frame so the two deltas do not
        // coexist with the credit call above.
        _settlePrincipal(
            poolManager,
            data.key,
            callerDelta,
            feesAccrued,
            data.user,
            data.liquidityDelta,
            data.amount0Limit,
            data.amount1Limit
        );
    }

    /// @dev Settles both currencies' principal for a modify: `callerDelta - feesAccrued` per side,
    ///      after checking each side against the caller's stated bound.
    function _settlePrincipal(
        IPoolManager poolManager,
        PoolKey memory key,
        BalanceDelta callerDelta,
        BalanceDelta feesAccrued,
        address user,
        int256 liquidityDelta,
        uint128 amount0Limit,
        uint128 amount1Limit
    ) private {
        int128 principal0 = callerDelta.amount0() - feesAccrued.amount0();
        int128 principal1 = callerDelta.amount1() - feesAccrued.amount1();
        _checkBound(principal0, liquidityDelta, amount0Limit);
        _checkBound(principal1, liquidityDelta, amount1Limit);
        _resolvePrincipal(poolManager, key.currency0, principal0, user);
        _resolvePrincipal(poolManager, key.currency1, principal1, user);
    }

    /// @dev The caller's consent, checked against what the price actually decided.
    ///
    ///      A `liquidity` figure is not a price: the same liquidity costs `L*(1/sqrt(Pa) -
    ///      1/sqrt(Pb))` of currency0 with the price at or below the range and `L*(sqrt(Pb) -
    ///      sqrt(Pa))` of currency1 at or above it, against a roughly even split mid-range. So a
    ///      price move between signing and execution silently converts the whole position into one
    ///      leg at a price nobody agreed to — and the pull was previously bounded only by the
    ///      caller's ERC-20 allowance. Worst on a freshly initialized pool, where there is no
    ///      liquidity to cross, so walking the price to any target consumes no input and pays no
    ///      fee: the manipulation is free, not merely cheap, which makes the FIRST deposit into a
    ///      new pool the highest-value target in the system.
    ///
    ///      Checked per currency, in the direction the action moves value:
    ///        - adding liquidity, the caller PAYS, so the bound is a ceiling;
    ///        - removing it, the caller RECEIVES, so the bound is a floor.
    ///      A zero delta is an internal fee sync that moves no principal, and is exempt.
    function _checkBound(int128 principal, int256 liquidityDelta, uint128 limit) private pure {
        if (liquidityDelta > 0) {
            // widen before negating: -int128 panics on type(int128).min
            // negating a NEGATIVE int128 inside int256 is always positive, so the uint256 cast is exact
            // forge-lint: disable-next-line(unsafe-typecast)
            uint256 paid = principal < 0 ? uint256(-int256(principal)) : 0;
            if (paid > limit) revert PrincipalAboveMax(paid, limit);
        } else if (liquidityDelta < 0) {
            // guarded positive on the same line, so the uint256 cast is exact
            // forge-lint: disable-next-line(unsafe-typecast)
            uint256 received = principal > 0 ? uint256(int256(principal)) : 0;
            if (received < limit) revert PrincipalBelowMin(received, limit);
        }
    }

    /// @dev Redeems held claims into real tokens for `to`. Kept off the withdraw path.
    function _redeem(IPoolManager poolManager, Currency currency, uint256 amount, address to) private {
        if (amount == 0) return;
        poolManager.burn(address(this), currency.toId(), amount);
        poolManager.take(currency, to, amount);
    }

    /// @notice Burns the ledger's claims and pays `to` in real tokens, via a PAYOUT unlock. A
    ///         failure here (paused/blocklisted token, blocked recipient) affects only this claim,
    ///         never principal. The hook's `_payout` is a one-line call into this.
    function payout(
        IPoolManager poolManager,
        Currency currency0,
        Currency currency1,
        uint256 amount0,
        uint256 amount1,
        address to
    ) internal {
        if (amount0 == 0 && amount1 == 0) return;
        poolManager.unlock(
            abi.encode(Action.PAYOUT, abi.encode(PayoutData(currency0, currency1, amount0, amount1, to)))
        );
    }

    /// @notice Pushes a currency's accrued staked-share revenue to the distributor — or, for the
    ///         protocol token, to the dedicated FF sink, since the epoch distributor treats every FF
    ///         it holds as the emission tranche and would strand fee revenue arriving there.
    function sweepDistributor(
        mapping(Currency => uint256) storage distributorOwed,
        IPoolManager poolManager,
        Currency currency,
        address distributor,
        address distributorFF,
        address ffFeeSink
    ) internal {
        uint256 amount = distributorOwed[currency];
        if (amount == 0) return;
        // `distributorFF != address(0)` is load-bearing, not redundant: NATIVE currency unwraps to
        // address(0) and `validateDistributor` caches address(0) whenever the distributor is cleared,
        // so without it the "is this the protocol token?" test also answers yes for native ETH and
        // routes native revenue to the FF sink. Unreachable today only because `FablesNativeETH`
        // overrides the native branch before this line and every other hook refuses native pools —
        // i.e. the safety lives in a different contract. Pinning it here makes it local.
        address to =
            (distributorFF != address(0) && Currency.unwrap(currency) == distributorFF) ? ffFeeSink : distributor;
        if (to == address(0)) return; // unset sink: the accrual simply keeps waiting
        distributorOwed[currency] = 0;
        payout(poolManager, currency, currency, amount, 0, to);
        emit DistributorSwept(currency, to, amount);
    }

    /// @notice Pushes a currency's accrued claim-fee revenue to the recipient. Pull-shaped: a
    ///         blocked recipient cannot wedge anyone else's sweep.
    function sweepClaimFee(
        mapping(Currency => uint256) storage claimFeeOwed,
        IPoolManager poolManager,
        Currency currency,
        address claimFeeRecipient
    ) internal {
        uint256 amount = claimFeeOwed[currency];
        if (claimFeeRecipient == address(0) || amount == 0) return;
        claimFeeOwed[currency] = 0;
        payout(poolManager, currency, currency, amount, 0, claimFeeRecipient);
        emit ClaimFeeSwept(currency, claimFeeRecipient, amount);
    }

    /// @dev Settles one currency's principal: a negative delta is owed BY the user (pulled and
    ///      settled to the pool manager), a positive delta is paid TO the user.
    // `user` is not attacker-chosen. It is `CallbackData.user`, reachable only through
    // `FablesLedger.unlockCallback`, which refuses any caller but the PoolManager. There are THREE
    // constructions of CallbackData, not two: `msg.sender` (deposit), `address(this)` (sync), and
    // `to` in `_withdrawTo` — which IS caller-supplied and may be any address. An earlier version
    // of this comment enumerated only the first two, which is the wrong basis for the suppression
    // below.
    //
    // The real invariant is a property of the withdraw path rather than of the writers: a removal
    // has `liquidityDelta < 0`, and v4-core's `SqrtPriceMath.getAmount0Delta`/`getAmount1Delta`
    // return a POSITIVE amount for negative liquidity, so `principal = callerDelta - feesAccrued`
    // is non-negative and `_resolvePrincipal` can only ever reach the `poolManager.take(currency,
    // user, ...)` branch. The `safeTransferFrom(user, ...)` branch is unreachable with a
    // third-party `user`. (`_checkBound` does NOT establish this — with `liquidityDelta < 0` and
    // `amountNMin == 0` a negative principal would pass it silently.)
    // Proven adversarially by test_fuzz_aDepositIsPaidForByTheCallerAndNobodyElse, which asserts
    // the ATTACKER's balance falls and a funded, approved victim's does not.
    // slither-disable-next-line arbitrary-send-erc20
    function _resolvePrincipal(IPoolManager poolManager, Currency currency, int128 amount, address user) private {
        if (amount < 0) {
            // widen before negating: -int128 panics on type(int128).min; the negation of a negative
            // int128 is always a positive int256, so the uint256 cast is exact
            // forge-lint: disable-next-line(unsafe-typecast)
            uint256 owed = uint256(-int256(amount));
            // Sync FIRST for both branches — the CurrencySettler-canonical order. For native it
            // clears the synced-currency slot so `settle` reads the attached value; skipping it
            // and relying on "no ERC20 was synced yet this unlock" would make correctness hinge
            // on resolution ORDER (native leg before ERC20 leg), a coupling nobody would see.
            poolManager.sync(currency);
            if (currency.isAddressZero()) {
                // The hook prepaid: `deposit` requires `msg.value >= amount0Max` and `_checkBound`
                // caps `owed` at `amount0Max`, so the balance this spends from can never be short.
                // Only a native-capable hook can reach here — every other hook's `_checkKey`
                // refuses native pools before an unlock is ever taken.
                poolManager.settle{value: owed}();
            } else {
                IERC20(Currency.unwrap(currency)).safeTransferFrom(user, address(poolManager), owed);
                poolManager.settle();
            }
        } else if (amount > 0) {
            // `take` moves native and ERC20 alike — the PoolManager itself pays the recipient.
            // forge-lint: disable-next-line(unsafe-typecast)
            poolManager.take(currency, user, uint256(uint128(amount)));
        }
    }
}
